window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["imhans-2020"] = {"id": "imhans-2020", "title": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2020", "fullTitle": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2020, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, from a coaching-book reprint)", "sub": "59 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The occurrence of \"Illusory conjunctions\" is evidence confirming the:", "options": {"a": "Pandemonium model", "b": "Prototype theory", "c": "Feature Integration theory", "d": "Bottleneck model"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: illusory conjunctions are the classic evidence for the feature integration theory of Anne Treisman and Garry Gelade (1980). The theory says that seeing an object happens in two stages. In the first, pre-attentive stage, simple features such as colour, orientation, curvature and size are registered automatically and in parallel across the whole visual field, each in its own feature map. In the second stage, focused attention moves to one location at a time and binds the features found there into a single object; attention is the glue. If attention is overloaded or diverted, the features are still registered but float free, so they can be combined wrongly. Anne Treisman and Hilary Schmidt (1982) briefly flashed three coloured letters (for example a green N, a red X and a blue O) flanked by two black digits, which had to be reported first; people often reported a red N or a blue X, an object that was never shown. These errors are illusory conjunctions. The same theory explains visual search: a target defined by one feature pops out, while a target defined by a conjunction of features needs slow, serial search. Example: Riya glances at a busy street while chatting and later remembers a yellow scooter, when the scooter was black and the yellow belonged to a taxi beside it. Option c is right because only feature integration theory says features are coded separately and need attention to be bound, which is exactly what an illusory conjunction shows when binding fails. Exam trap: Pandemonium also breaks patterns into features, so students pick it, but it has no attention-binding stage and does not predict miscombined features.", "topic": "Feature integration theory", "fig": "Anne Treisman and Garry Gelade"}, {"q": "The ERP (Event-Related Potential) component that occurs when the form of a sentence is incorrect:", "options": {"a": "P600", "b": "N400", "c": "P400", "d": "N600"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the P600 is the event-related potential (ERP) component evoked when the grammatical form of a sentence is wrong. An ERP is the small voltage change in the EEG that is time-locked to a stimulus. Because a single trial is buried in background EEG, hundreds of trials are averaged, and the waves that remain are named by polarity (P for positive, N for negative) and by latency in milliseconds. In language research two components matter most. The N400 is a negative wave peaking about 400 ms after a word whose meaning does not fit the sentence, first reported by Marta Kutas and Steven Hillyard (1980) with sentences such as 'He spread the warm bread with socks'. The P600, also called the syntactic positive shift, is a positive wave beginning about 500 ms and peaking near 600 ms after a word that breaks the rules of grammar, such as a wrong verb agreement, or a garden-path sentence that has to be re-parsed. Lee Osterhout and Phillip Holcomb (1992) and Peter Hagoort, Colin Brown and Jolanda Groothusen (1993) described it. Example: if Kabir reads 'The boys eats mangoes', a P600 appears at 'eats'; if he reads 'The boys ate their shoes for lunch', an N400 appears at 'shoes'. Option a is right because the form of a sentence means its syntax, and syntactic violations produce the P600. Exam trap: the N400 is the better-known language component, so students choose it; remember N400 for meaning (semantics) and P600 for grammar (syntax).", "topic": "ERP components in language", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Research on the phenomenon of memory consolidation can be traced to the pioneering work of:", "options": {"a": "Hermann Ebbinghaus", "b": "Muller and Pilzecker", "c": "Franciscus Donders", "d": "William James"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: research on memory consolidation goes back to Georg Elias Müller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900), who coined the term. Consolidation is the process by which a new, fragile memory trace becomes stable and long lasting over time. Müller and Pilzecker had people learn lists of nonsense syllable pairs and found that recall was poorer when a second list was learned soon after the first than when a rest interval came in between. They argued that the neural activity set up by learning carries on for a while after practice ends (perseveration) and that this continued activity fixes the trace; new learning during that window disrupts it. This was the perseveration-consolidation hypothesis, and it was also the first clear demonstration of retroactive interference. Later evidence fits the idea: a head injury or electroconvulsive therapy wipes out memories of the minutes before the event, while older memories survive, and Théodule Ribot (1881) had already noted that recent memories are lost before old ones. Today consolidation is studied at the synapse (protein synthesis) and at the systems level (hippocampus to cortex, helped by sleep). Example: Meera revises her polity notes and then immediately crams economics; next day her polity recall is weaker than if she had rested, because the second session disturbed consolidation. Option b is right because Müller and Pilzecker both introduced the term consolidation and ran the first experiments on it. Exam trap: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) is the famous memory pioneer, so students pick him, but he measured forgetting and savings, not consolidation.", "topic": "Origins of memory consolidation", "fig": "Georg Elias Müller and Alfons Pilzecker"}, {"q": "In a simple linear regression equation, b₀ represents the:", "options": {"a": "Estimated or predicted response", "b": "Estimated intercept", "c": "Estimated slope", "d": "Explanatory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in a simple linear regression equation, b0 is the estimated intercept. Simple linear regression fits a straight line that predicts one variable (Y, the response or criterion) from one other variable (X, the explanatory or predictor variable). The fitted equation is written Y-hat = b0 + b1X. Y-hat is the estimated or predicted response. b1 is the estimated slope: how much Y-hat changes for every one-unit rise in X. b0 is the estimated intercept, the constant: the predicted value of Y when X equals 0, the point where the line crosses the Y axis. Both are found by the method of least squares, which chooses the line that makes the sum of the squared vertical distances (residuals) between the points and the line as small as possible. The slope is b1 = r(SDy / SDx), and the intercept is then b0 = mean of Y minus b1 times mean of X, so the line always passes through the point given by the two means. Greek letters (beta0 and beta1) stand for the population values; b0 and b1 are the sample estimates, which is why the options say 'estimated'. Example: Arjun's teacher finds that exam score = 35 + 5 x hours of study. Here 35 is b0, the score predicted for a student who studies for 0 hours, and 5 is b1, five extra marks for each extra hour. Option b is right because b0 is the constant term that fixes where the line starts on the Y axis. Exam trap: distractors swap slope and intercept; the slope is the coefficient that multiplies X, while the intercept stands alone.", "topic": "Regression intercept", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The F+ response in the Rorschach protocol exceeding 80% is indicative of:", "options": {"a": "Greater ego strength", "b": "Severe psychopathology", "c": "Impulsiveness", "d": "Internalization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: an F+ percentage above 80 in a Rorschach record is read as greater ego strength, that is, sound reality testing and ego control. In Rorschach scoring each response is coded for its determinant, the feature of the blot that produced it. F means the response was based on form (shape) alone, with no colour, shading or movement. Each pure form response is then judged for form quality: F+ if the percept fits the shape of the blot well, F minus if the fit is poor or arbitrary. Hermann Rorschach (1921) introduced this scoring, and F+% (good pure form responses divided by all pure form responses, times 100) became, in the systems of Bruno Klopfer (1942) and Samuel Beck (1944), the main index of how accurately a person sees reality. A high F+% (about 80 and above) means the person checks inner ideas against the outer world and keeps impulses under control, the classical meaning of an intact, strong ego. A low F+% (below about 60 to 70) means percepts are driven by inner preoccupations, as in psychosis or organic brain disorder. Example: Kabir, a stable student, sees a bat and two dancers where the shapes really fit, giving an F+% near 85. Option a is right because a high F+% shows accurate, controlled perception, which is ego strength. Exam trap: severe psychopathology is the opposite pole; it goes with a low F+%, not a high one. Key note: a very high F+% together with a high proportion of pure form responses (F%) and few colour or movement responses is read as rigid over-control and constriction, so very high values are not simply better; but of these options, greater ego strength is the textbook reading, and a stands.", "topic": "Rorschach F+ percentage", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "A Statistical procedure for determining the relationship between two or more independent variables and two or more dependent variables is:", "options": {"a": "Cluster analysis", "b": "Multiple regression", "c": "Canonical analysis", "d": "Survival analysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: canonical analysis (canonical correlation analysis) is the procedure for relating two or more independent variables to two or more dependent variables at the same time. It was developed by Harold Hotelling (1936). Ordinary correlation links one X with one Y, and multiple regression links several Xs with one Y. Canonical analysis goes a step further: it takes a whole set of predictors and a whole set of criteria, forms a weighted sum (a linear combination, called a canonical variate) of each set, and chooses the weights so that the correlation between the two sums is as large as possible. That correlation is the first canonical correlation. The procedure then finds a second pair of variates, uncorrelated with the first pair, and so on, up to the number of variables in the smaller set. Each canonical correlation is tested for significance (often with Wilks' lambda), and the loadings show which variables carry each link. Multiple regression, ANOVA, MANOVA and discriminant analysis can all be treated as special cases of it. Example: Ananya measures three family variables (warmth, conflict, parental education) and four adolescent outcomes (self-esteem, grades, anxiety, peer relations). Canonical analysis tells her how the family set as a whole relates to the outcome set as a whole, instead of running twelve separate correlations. Option c is right because it is the only option built for many predictors and many outcomes together. Exam trap: multiple regression also handles several variables, but it allows only one dependent variable; with two or more DVs you need canonical analysis (or MANOVA when the IVs are groups).", "topic": "Canonical correlation analysis", "fig": "Harold Hotelling"}, {"q": "The taming effect in Kluver-Bucy syndrome is attributed to lesions in the following:", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus", "b": "Presubiculum", "c": "Fornix", "d": "Amygdala"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the taming effect in Klüver-Bucy syndrome is attributed to loss of the amygdala. Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both anterior temporal lobes of rhesus monkeys. Previously wild, fearful animals became tame and placid: they approached snakes and humans without fear. The monkeys also showed psychic blindness or visual agnosia (they saw objects but did not recognise their meaning), hyperorality (putting everything in the mouth), changes in diet, hypersexuality and hypermetamorphosis (an urge to attend to and touch everything in sight). Because the lesions were large, the next question was which structure caused which sign. Lawrence Weiskrantz (1956) made smaller lesions limited to the amygdala and found that amygdala removal alone produced the taming. This fits the amygdala's role in detecting threat, in fear conditioning and in giving objects their emotional value. In humans the syndrome appears after herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia. Example: if Arjun's laboratory monkey were given bilateral amygdala lesions, it would stop shrieking at a rubber snake and might even pick it up. Option d is right because the amygdala is the structure whose removal abolishes fear and aggression, the core of the taming effect. Exam trap: the hippocampus lies beside the amygdala in the same temporal lobe and was also removed, so students choose it; its loss affects memory, not fear. Key note: the paper prints 'training effect', which is plainly a misprint of 'taming effect' (placidity and loss of fear), the reading shown in the stem here; on that reading, d stands.", "topic": "Klüver-Bucy taming effect", "fig": "Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements regarding Collins and Quillian's model are true? 1. It is a hierarchical model 2. Back propagation is a feature of this model 3. The model explains the typicality effect 4. The lexical decision task validates the model", "options": {"a": "2 & 3 only", "b": "2 & 4 only", "c": "1 & 4 only", "d": "1 & 3 only"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 4 are true. Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) proposed the hierarchical network model of semantic memory. Concepts are nodes arranged in levels (ANIMAL above BIRD above CANARY) joined by 'is a' links, and each property is stored once, at the highest node it applies to (cognitive economy): 'has skin' at ANIMAL, 'can fly' at BIRD, 'is yellow' at CANARY. They tested it with sentence verification: people took longer to verify 'A canary has skin' than 'A canary can fly', as if moving up more levels. So statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: back propagation is the learning rule of connectionist (parallel distributed processing) networks, described by David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams (1986). Statement 3 is false: the model could not explain the typicality effect, that 'A robin is a bird' is verified faster than 'A penguin is a bird' though both are one level apart (Lance Rips, Edward Shoben and Edward Smith, 1973). That failure led to the feature comparison model (Edward Smith, Edward Shoben and Lance Rips, 1974) and the spreading activation model (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975). Statement 4: semantic priming in the lexical decision task (David Meyer and Roger Schvaneveldt, 1971), where 'nurse' is recognised faster after 'doctor', is the classic evidence for network models. Example: Meera says yes to 'A sparrow is a bird' faster than to 'An ostrich is a bird', the typicality effect the model cannot explain. Option c is right because only 1 and 4 survive. Exam trap: students credit the model with typicality, its best-known failure. Key note: strictly, Collins and Quillian used sentence verification, and lexical decision priming supports semantic networks in general (the 1975 revision most of all), so statement 4 is the loosest; but 3 is plainly false and 2 belongs to connectionism, so 1 and 4 is the only defensible pair, and c stands.", "topic": "Collins and Quillian network", "fig": "Allan Collins and Ross Quillian"}, {"q": "Akinetic mutism results from lesions in which part of the frontal lobe:", "options": {"a": "Dorsolateral", "b": "Medial", "c": "Orbito frontal", "d": "Prefrontal"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: akinetic mutism results from lesions of the medial frontal lobe. Akinetic mutism is a state in which the patient is awake and follows the examiner with the eyes, yet shows almost no spontaneous movement or speech. Sensory and motor pathways are intact, so with strong urging the patient may move or say a word; what is lost is the drive to act, an extreme form of abulia (lack of will and initiative). Hugh Cairns and colleagues (1941) named the state in a patient with a cyst in the third ventricle. The typical lesion is bilateral damage to the medial frontal cortex, especially the anterior cingulate cortex and the supplementary motor area, often from infarcts of both anterior cerebral arteries (which supply the medial surface of the hemispheres) or a midline tumour. The anterior cingulate links motivation with action, which is why its loss leaves a frozen but conscious person. Learn the three prefrontal syndromes as a set: dorsolateral damage gives the dysexecutive syndrome, orbitofrontal damage gives disinhibition, and medial damage gives apathy, abulia and, at its worst, akinetic mutism. Example: after strokes in both anterior cerebral arteries, Rohan lies with his eyes open and tracks his mother around the room, but does not speak or move unless prompted again and again. Option b is right because the medial frontal and anterior cingulate regions supply the drive to start movement and speech. Exam trap: 'prefrontal' sounds right because the syndrome is frontal, but it is the umbrella term for all three regions, not the specific site.", "topic": "Akinetic mutism lesion site", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The testing effects occurring for a within-subject variable are overcome through:", "options": {"a": "Solomon four groups", "b": "Double-blind design", "c": "Using a control group", "d": "Observing the participants unobtrusively"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: testing effects are overcome by the Solomon four-group design, introduced by Richard Solomon (1949). A testing effect occurs when taking a pretest itself changes scores on the posttest, through practice, familiarity or sensitisation to the topic. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) list testing as a threat to internal validity, and they add a related threat to external validity: the pretest can interact with the treatment, making people more responsive to it, so results may not apply to people who were never pretested. The pretest is a within-subject element, because the same person is measured twice. The Solomon design crosses pretest (yes or no) with treatment (yes or no): group 1 gets pretest, treatment and posttest; group 2 gets pretest and posttest only; group 3 gets treatment and posttest; group 4 gets the posttest alone. Comparing pretested with unpretested groups estimates the testing effect, and comparing the treatment effect across them reveals the pretest-by-treatment interaction, so each can be measured and separated. Its cost is that it needs twice as many participants. Example: Riya tests whether an anti-stigma talk changes students' attitudes to mental illness. The pretest questionnaire might itself make students think about stigma; the two unpretested groups show how much of the change came from the questionnaire. Option a is right because only this design measures testing effects directly instead of hoping they cancel out. Exam trap: an ordinary control group also takes the pretest, so it equalises the main testing effect but cannot detect the pretest-by-treatment interaction.", "topic": "Solomon four-group design", "fig": "Richard Solomon"}, {"q": "Which of the following can be used to identify interaction effects when the predictor and moderator variables are nominal, and the dependent variable is a score variable?", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "Discriminant Analysis", "c": "Chi-square", "d": "Logistic Regression"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: when the predictor and the moderator are both nominal (categorical) and the dependent variable is a score, the interaction is tested with ANOVA. A moderator is a variable that changes the direction or strength of the relation between a predictor and an outcome; it tells us when, or for whom, an effect holds. Reuben Baron and David Kenny (1986) set out how to test moderation, and the method depends on the level of measurement of the variables. If both predictor and moderator are categorical and the outcome is continuous, moderation is simply the interaction term in a factorial (for example 2 by 2) analysis of variance. The main effect of each factor is tested, and a significant interaction F shows that the effect of one factor differs across the levels of the other, which is what moderation means. If the predictor or the moderator is continuous, moderated multiple regression is used instead: the two variables are entered, then their product term, and a significant gain in variance explained shows moderation. Example: Kabir compares therapy with a waiting list (the predictor) in men and women (the moderator), with depression scores as the outcome. A two-way ANOVA tells him whether therapy works, whether the sexes differ, and, through the interaction, whether therapy helps one sex more than the other. Option a is right because ANOVA is the standard test of an interaction between nominal factors on a score DV. Exam trap: chi-square handles nominal variables, so it tempts, but it needs a nominal outcome as well, and here the outcome is a score.", "topic": "Testing moderation with ANOVA", "fig": "Reuben Baron and David Kenny"}, {"q": "The motor pathway that controls hand and finger movements:", "options": {"a": "Cortico-bulbar pathway", "b": "Cortico-spinal pathway", "c": "Ventromedial pathway", "d": "Rubrospinal tract"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the corticospinal pathway, specifically the lateral corticospinal tract, controls hand and finger movements. This tract begins in the primary motor cortex of the precentral gyrus, including the giant Betz cells of layer V, and descends through the internal capsule and the cerebral peduncles to the pyramids of the medulla, which is why it is also called the pyramidal tract. At the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla about 85 to 90 per cent of its fibres cross, so each hemisphere moves the opposite side of the body. The crossed fibres run in the lateral column of the spinal cord, and in primates many of them synapse directly on the motor neurons that supply the muscles of the fingers. This direct link allows fractionated, independent finger movements, such as writing or picking up a needle. Monkeys whose pyramidal tracts have been cut can still walk, climb and grasp with the whole hand, but they lose the ability to move the fingers one at a time. The hand also has a large share of the motor homunculus. Example: when Meera threads a needle or types a text message, her lateral corticospinal tract is doing the fine work. Option b is right because the lateral corticospinal tract is the only descending pathway with direct connections to the finger motor neurons. Exam trap: the rubrospinal tract also controls limb movements, including the hand, so students choose it; but it moves the forearm and hand as a unit and cannot give independent finger control.", "topic": "Corticospinal tract", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The two-point code is a unique feature of:", "options": {"a": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)", "b": "Beck Depression Inventory (BDI)", "c": "Halstead-Reitan Battery", "d": "Luria Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the two-point code is a feature of the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI), published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). The MMPI has 10 clinical scales, numbered 1 to 0 (1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania, 0 Social Introversion), plus validity scales. Raw scores are turned into T scores, and clinicians read the whole profile rather than single scales. In code-type interpretation, the numbers of the two highest elevated clinical scales are written as a two-point code: for example 2-7 (anxious, worried depression), 4-9 (impulsive, antisocial acting out) or 1-3 (physical complaints under stress). Research has gathered the typical traits of people with each code, so a code works as shorthand for a well-studied personality pattern; three-point codes extend the idea. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 shows Depression at T 78 and Psychasthenia at T 72 as his two highest scales, so his profile is coded 2-7, suggesting a tense, self-critical, depressed man. Option a is right because only the MMPI reports results as codes built from the numbers of the highest clinical scales. Exam trap: the Luria-Nebraska also has numbered clinical scales, so students choose it, but its standard reading compares each scale with a critical level for brain damage; the two-point code is the hallmark of the MMPI.", "topic": "MMPI two-point codes", "fig": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "The Go/No-Go test is a bed side test for assessing the function of:", "options": {"a": "Temporal lobe", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Parietal lobe", "d": "Occipital lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Go/No-Go test is a bedside test of frontal lobe function. The patient must respond to one signal and hold back the response to another, for example: 'tap once when I tap once; do not tap when I tap twice'. The go trials are easy; the hard part is the no-go trial, which needs response inhibition, the ability to stop a prepared or habitual action. Response inhibition is an executive function that depends on the prefrontal cortex, especially the right inferior frontal gyrus and the pre-supplementary motor area, and on their links with the basal ganglia. Patients with frontal damage keep tapping on no-go trials or simply copy the examiner (echopraxia), showing a loss of inhibitory control. Alexander Luria (1966) used such conflicting and go/no-go instructions in his clinical examination of frontal patients, and the Frontal Assessment Battery of Bruno Dubois and colleagues (2000) includes a go/no-go item along with similarities, verbal fluency, the fist-edge-palm motor sequence, conflicting instructions and a test of grasping behaviour. Example: Kabir, who has had a frontal head injury, is asked to raise a finger at one tap and stay still at two; he raises it every time. Option b is right because the test probes the inhibition of a prepotent response, a classic frontal executive function. Exam trap: the task is sometimes mistaken for a test of attention or memory, and so of the temporal or parietal lobes, but its core deficit is failed inhibition, which is frontal.", "topic": "Go/No-Go frontal test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The assumption that pairs of scores from the same individuals in the population have equal variance is called:", "options": {"a": "Levene's assumption", "b": "Sphericity assumption", "c": "Independence assumption", "d": "Pairs assumption"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the assumption that pairs of scores from the same individuals have equal variance (of their differences) is the sphericity assumption. Sphericity belongs to repeated-measures (within-subjects) ANOVA, in which the same people are measured under three or more conditions. For every pair of conditions you can compute each person's difference score (condition A minus B, A minus C, B minus C). Sphericity requires that the population variances of all these difference scores are equal. When it holds, the F ratio follows the F distribution correctly; when it is violated, the F test becomes too liberal and Type I errors rise. It is tested with Mauchly's test (John Mauchly, 1940); a significant result means the assumption is violated, and the degrees of freedom are then reduced by the Greenhouse-Geisser correction (more conservative) or the Huynh-Feldt correction (less so), or a multivariate test such as MANOVA is used instead. Sphericity cannot be violated with only two conditions, because there is only one set of difference scores. Example: Meera measures anxiety in the same 20 clients at intake, after 6 weeks and after 12 weeks. If the spread of the intake to 6 week changes is very different from the spread of the 6 to 12 week changes, sphericity is violated. Option b is right because sphericity is exactly the equal-variance condition on difference scores within the same people. Exam trap: Levene's test also concerns equal variances, so it tempts, but it compares separate, independent groups, not repeated scores from the same individuals.", "topic": "Sphericity assumption", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Weber fraction for certain sensory dimensions is given below. Indicate the most sensitive dimension:", "options": {"a": "0.083", "b": "0.048", "c": "0.036", "d": "0.013"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the dimension with the Weber fraction 0.013 is the most sensitive. Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834) found that the just noticeable difference (JND), the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect half the time, is not a fixed amount but a constant fraction of the starting (standard) stimulus. Weber's law is written delta I / I = k, where I is the standard intensity, delta I is the JND and k is the Weber fraction (Weber's constant) for that dimension. The fraction differs from one sensory dimension to another but stays roughly constant within a dimension across the middle range of intensities. Because k is the proportion by which a stimulus must change before the change is noticed, a smaller fraction means a smaller change is enough, so the sense is more finely tuned. A fraction of 0.013 means a change of only 1.3 per cent is noticed; 0.083 means a change of 8.3 per cent is needed. Gustav Theodor Fechner (1860) built on this to derive his logarithmic law, S = k log I, and founded psychophysics. Example: starting from a standard of 100 units, Arjun detects a change of only 1.3 units on a dimension with k = 0.013, but on a dimension with k = 0.083 he needs a change of 8.3 units before he notices anything. Option d is right because 0.013 is the smallest fraction, so the smallest proportional change is detected. Exam trap: students assume a bigger number means a stronger sense and pick 0.083; in Weber's law, the bigger the fraction, the poorer the discrimination.", "topic": "Weber fraction and sensitivity", "fig": "Ernst Heinrich Weber"}, {"q": "The intelligence test that embodies two theoretical models of intelligence, viz, the approach of Luria and the CHC model is:", "options": {"a": "Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children-II", "b": "Detroit Tests of Learning Aptitude-4", "c": "Stanford Binet Fifth Edition (SB5)", "d": "Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children, Second Edition (KABC-II), by Alan Kaufman and Nadeen Kaufman (2004), is built on two theoretical models of intelligence at once. The original K-ABC (1983) rested on the neuropsychological model of Alexander Luria (1973), especially sequential processing (handling information in serial order, as in repeating a digit string) and simultaneous processing (integrating many pieces at once, as in completing a picture). The KABC-II, for ages 3 to 18, keeps a Luria model with four scales (Sequential, Simultaneous, Learning and Planning), which yields the Mental Processing Index. It also offers a Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which reads the same scales as short-term memory (Gsm), visual processing (Gv), long-term storage and retrieval (Glr) and fluid reasoning (Gf), and adds a Knowledge scale for crystallised ability (Gc), yielding the Fluid-Crystallized Index. The examiner chooses the model before testing: the Luria model is preferred when a child's language or cultural background would make knowledge-based items unfair, the CHC model when the child shares the mainstream language and culture. A Nonverbal Index is also available. Example: for Arjun, whose home language differs from the language of testing, the psychologist picks the Luria model so that vocabulary and general knowledge do not pull his score down. Option a is right because the KABC-II is the only test listed that is explicitly built on both Luria's model and CHC theory. Exam trap: the SB5 also draws on CHC theory, so it tempts, but it uses CHC alone, with no Luria framework.", "topic": "KABC-II dual model", "fig": "Alan Kaufman and Nadeen Kaufman"}, {"q": "The term double depression is used to describe:", "options": {"a": "A severe bout of major depression", "b": "Major depression superimposed on dysthymia", "c": "Recurrent episodes of major depression within a 2-month period", "d": "Medical illness with a superimposed episode of major depression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: double depression means a major depressive episode superimposed on dysthymia. Dysthymia (now persistent depressive disorder in DSM-5) is a chronic, milder depression: depressed mood on most days for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children and adolescents), with two or more symptoms such as poor appetite, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or hopelessness, and never more than 2 months free of symptoms. When a full major depressive episode develops on top of this long-standing low mood, the person has two depressions at once. Martin Keller and Robert Shapiro (1982) coined the term. Follow-up studies by Martin Keller and colleagues showed that such patients often recover from the major episode fairly quickly but fall back to their dysthymic baseline rather than to full health, and they relapse more often, so the outlook is worse than for major depression alone. DSM-5 folds the pattern into persistent depressive disorder, using the specifier 'with intermittent major depressive episodes' or 'with persistent major depressive episode'. Treatment usually combines antidepressants with psychotherapy, such as the Cognitive Behavioral Analysis System of Psychotherapy (CBASP) of James McCullough (2000), designed for chronic depression. Example: Meera has felt low and joyless for four years but kept working; after losing her job she develops severe depression with insomnia and suicidal thoughts. She has double depression. Option b is right because the term names exactly this layering of a major episode on chronic dysthymia. Exam trap: 'a severe bout of major depression' sounds double in strength, but the term refers to two disorders together, not to severity.", "topic": "Double depression", "fig": "Martin Keller and Robert Shapiro"}, {"q": "The set of ethical guidelines that researchers in Psychology must adhere to while conducting studies on human participants is provided by:", "options": {"a": "Nuremberg Code", "b": "APA ethics code", "c": "Belmont Report", "d": "M'Naughten Rule"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ethical guidelines psychologists must follow when they study human participants are set out in the APA ethics code, the Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association. The first APA code appeared in 1953; the current version dates from 2002, with amendments in 2010 and 2016. It has five general principles (beneficence and nonmaleficence; fidelity and responsibility; integrity; justice; respect for people's rights and dignity) and a set of enforceable standards. The research standards cover approval by an institutional review board, informed consent (including the right to withdraw without penalty), the narrow cases where consent may be waived, inducements, deception only when the study's value justifies it and no non-deceptive method exists, prompt debriefing, humane care of animals, honest reporting and no plagiarism; privacy and confidentiality have their own section. Other bodies have similar codes, such as the British Psychological Society, and Indian universities use institutional ethics committees that apply the same principles. Example: before Kabir interviews college students about self-harm, the APA code requires him to obtain ethics committee approval, explain the study and the right to stop at any time, protect the students' identities, and give helpline numbers during debriefing. Option b is right because the APA code is the professional code written specifically for psychologists, including their research with people. Exam trap: the Belmont Report also concerns research ethics and is often quoted in psychology texts, but it is a US government report for all human research, not psychology's own code.", "topic": "APA ethics code", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In an ROC curve, the greater the d' value and more bowed the curve, then:", "options": {"a": "Higher the false alarm and lower the hit rate", "b": "Higher the hit rate and lower the rate of false alarm", "c": "Higher the misses and lower the false alarm", "d": "Higher the correct rejection and higher the false alarm"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the greater the d' and the more bowed the ROC curve, the higher the hit rate and the lower the false alarm rate. Signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966) treats detection as a decision under uncertainty. On each trial there is either noise alone or a signal added to noise, and the internal evidence in each case forms an overlapping normal distribution. d' (d prime) is the sensitivity index: the distance between the means of the noise and signal-plus-noise distributions, in standard deviation units. The observer also sets a criterion (beta), saying yes whenever the evidence is above it. Four outcomes follow: hit (yes to a signal), miss (no to a signal), false alarm (yes to noise) and correct rejection (no to noise). The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve plots the hit rate against the false alarm rate as the criterion moves from strict to lax. If d' is 0, the two distributions coincide and the curve is the diagonal: hits rise only as fast as false alarms, which is chance. As d' grows, the distributions separate and the curve bows towards the top-left corner, so at any false alarm rate the hit rate is higher. Example: Riya, an experienced radiologist, has a larger d' for spotting tumours than a trainee; her ROC curve bows further, so she catches more tumours while raising fewer false alarms. Option b is right because a more bowed curve means more hits for fewer false alarms. Exam trap: shifting the criterion moves hits and false alarms together along one curve; only a greater d' lifts the whole curve towards the top-left.", "topic": "ROC curve and d prime", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a researcher sets a significance level (alpha level), p < 0.05 changed to a more stringent level p <= 0.01 then:", "options": {"a": "Both Type I and Type II error becomes higher", "b": "Type I error becomes lower and Type II error becomes higher", "c": "Both Type I and Type II error becomes lower", "d": "Type I error becomes higher and Type II error becomes lower"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: making alpha stricter, from 0.05 to 0.01, lowers the risk of a Type I error but raises the risk of a Type II error. In significance testing, a Type I error (alpha error) is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true, a false positive. A Type II error (beta error) is retaining a null hypothesis that is actually false, a real effect missed. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) built this two-error framework; Ronald Fisher (1925) had popularised 0.05 as a convenient cut-off. Alpha is chosen by the researcher and is exactly the probability of a Type I error. Beta is not chosen; it follows from sample size, effect size, variability and alpha itself. Power is 1 minus beta, the chance of detecting a real effect. Suppose Arjun tests whether a new relaxation programme lowers exam anxiety. At alpha 0.05 (two-tailed) his z must pass 1.96; at 0.01 it must pass 2.58. The rejection region shrinks, so he will rarely declare a false effect, but a real, modest effect now more often falls short of the cut-off and is missed. When only alpha moves, the two errors trade off like a seesaw. Option b is right because tightening alpha reduces Type I errors and, with sample and effect unchanged, increases Type II errors and lowers power. The only way to lower both at once is to add information: a larger sample, a stronger manipulation or less measurement noise. Exam trap: examiners reverse the seesaw (option d describes loosening alpha to 0.10) or offer 'both lower', which needs a bigger sample, not a stricter alpha.", "topic": "Alpha level and error trade-off", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A component of the extrapyramidal system that plays an inhibitory role in spontaneous motor activity:", "options": {"a": "Caudate Nucleus", "b": "Globus Pallidus", "c": "Putamen", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the caudate nucleus is the extrapyramidal structure classically described as inhibiting spontaneous motor activity. The pyramidal system is the corticospinal tract, running from the motor cortex through the medullary pyramids to the spinal cord. The extrapyramidal system is the older label for the other motor structures, chiefly the basal ganglia: the caudate nucleus and putamen (together the striatum, which receives cortical input), the globus pallidus, and their partners the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra. These nuclei do not command muscles; they modulate the cortex through the thalamus, selecting some movements and suppressing others. Classic physiological psychology texts give the caudate a braking role. Electrical stimulation of the caudate in animals such as cats halts ongoing movement, the 'caudate arrest' response: an animal that is walking or eating simply stops. Caudate lesions do the opposite, giving restless hyperactivity and 'obstinate progression', the animal pressing forward against a wall. In Huntington's disease striatal neurons die, the caudate first, and unwanted dance-like movements (chorea) are released, which fits the same idea of lost inhibition. Option a is right because stimulation, lesion and clinical evidence all point to the caudate as the brake on spontaneous activity in the older extrapyramidal account the stem uses. Exam trap: the cerebellum is often slipped in as a motor structure, but it coordinates and times movement and is not part of the extrapyramidal system proper. Key note: in the modern circuit model (Garrett Alexander, Mahlon DeLong and Peter Strick, 1986; Roger Albin, Anne Young and John Penney, 1989), the internal globus pallidus is the GABAergic output that holds the thalamus in check, so some defend b. The stem's wording about spontaneous motor activity follows the older caudate-inhibition account, so a stands.", "topic": "Caudate and motor inhibition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Rescorla-Wagner model was developed to explain the phenomena of:", "options": {"a": "Second-order conditioning", "b": "Autoshaping", "c": "Blocking", "d": "Contiguity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Rescorla-Wagner model was developed to explain blocking. Before the late 1960s, conditioning was thought to need only contiguity, the conditioned stimulus (CS) and unconditioned stimulus (US) close together in time. Robert Rescorla (1968) showed that rats did not fear a tone if shocks were just as likely without it: what matters is contingency, how well the CS predicts the US. Leon Kamin (1969) then found blocking. Rats first learned that a noise predicted shock; then a noise plus light compound was followed by the same shock. Tested alone, the light produced almost no fear, although it had been paired with shock many times. Contiguity could not explain this. Robert Rescorla and Allan Wagner (1972) proposed that an animal learns only when surprised. On each trial the change in a CS's associative strength is proportional to the prediction error: the most the US can support (lambda) minus the summed strength of all stimuli present, scaled by salience and learning rate. In blocking the noise already predicts the shock fully, so the error is zero and the light gains nothing. Imagine Kabir's dog already salivating to a bell; adding a lamp to the bell before food teaches the dog nothing about the lamp. Option c is right because blocking was the puzzle the model was built to solve; it also predicts overshadowing, conditioned inhibition and overexpectation. It fails on latent inhibition (Robert Lubow and A. U. Moore, 1959), slower learning after unreinforced pre-exposure. Exam trap: 'contiguity' tempts because it is a classic conditioning term, but it is the old account the model replaced with surprise.", "topic": "Rescorla-Wagner model and blocking", "fig": "Robert Rescorla and Allan Wagner"}, {"q": "The part of the brain mediating the analgesic effects of opiate drugs:", "options": {"a": "Red nucleus", "b": "Periaqueductal gray", "c": "Substantia nigra", "d": "Thalamus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the periaqueductal grey (PAG) of the midbrain mediates much of the pain-relieving effect of opiate drugs such as morphine. The PAG is the grey matter surrounding the cerebral aqueduct, the narrow canal through the midbrain. Three lines of evidence tied analgesia to it. First, David Reynolds (1969) electrically stimulated the PAG in rats and could operate on their abdomens without anaesthetic, a finding called stimulation-produced analgesia. Second, Candace Pert and Solomon Snyder (1973) demonstrated opiate receptors in the brain, dense in the PAG, and John Hughes and Hans Kosterlitz (1975) isolated the enkephalins, the brain's own opioids. Third, tiny injections of morphine into the PAG relieve pain, while blocking opioid receptors there weakens the effect of morphine given to the whole body. The circuit, set out by Allan Basbaum and Howard Fields (1978), descends from the PAG to the serotonergic raphe nuclei of the medulla and then to the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, where interneurons release enkephalin and block incoming pain signals before they rise to the brain. When Riya is given morphine after an appendix operation, part of her relief comes from switching on this descending brake. Option b is right because the PAG is rich in opioid receptors and is the starting point of the descending pain-control pathway through which opiates act. Exam trap: the substantia nigra and red nucleus also lie in the midbrain, so remembering only 'a midbrain structure' can mislead; and the thalamus relays pain upward rather than suppressing it.", "topic": "PAG and opiate analgesia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "X-linked recessive conditions associated with mental retardation include all the following, EXCEPT:", "options": {"a": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy", "b": "Lesch-Nyhan syndrome", "c": "Adrenoleukodystrophy", "d": "Cri-du-chat syndrome"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: cri-du-chat syndrome is not an X-linked recessive condition; it is an autosomal chromosomal deletion. In X-linked recessive inheritance the faulty gene lies on the X chromosome. A female with one faulty copy is usually a healthy carrier, because her second X masks it; a male has only one X, so a single faulty copy produces the disorder. Such conditions therefore strike mainly boys and pass through carrier mothers. If Meera carries a faulty X-linked gene, each son has a 50% chance of being affected and each daughter a 50% chance of being a carrier. Three of the options follow this pattern and can impair intellect. Duchenne muscular dystrophy comes from mutations in the dystrophin gene on the X; boys develop progressive muscle weakness, and about a third also have cognitive impairment. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, described by Michael Lesch and William Nyhan (1964), is a deficiency of the enzyme HPRT, causing excess uric acid, dystonia, intellectual disability and striking self-biting. X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy comes from ABCD1 mutations; very long chain fatty acids build up, and the childhood cerebral form brings behavioural and cognitive decline as myelin breaks down. Cri-du-chat syndrome, identified by Jerome Lejeune (1963), is a deletion on the short arm of chromosome 5 (5p minus), so it affects both sexes; infants have a high, cat-like cry, a small head and severe intellectual disability. Option d is right because its cause lies on an autosome, not on the X. Exam trap: 'genetic' is not the same as 'X-linked'; deletion syndromes (cri-du-chat, Williams, Prader-Willi) are chromosomal errors, whereas fragile X, Lesch-Nyhan and Duchenne sit on the X.", "topic": "X-linked versus autosomal syndromes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Ability to repeat spoken words is preserved in:", "options": {"a": "Motor aphasia", "b": "Sensory aphasia", "c": "Transcortical motor aphasia", "d": "Conduction Aphasia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: repetition of spoken words is preserved in transcortical motor aphasia. Aphasia is a language disorder after brain damage, usually to the left hemisphere. Carl Wernicke (1874) and Ludwig Lichtheim (1885) classified the aphasias by three tests: fluency of spontaneous speech, comprehension, and repetition. Repetition depends on the perisylvian loop: Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus takes in the heard words, the arcuate fasciculus carries them forward, and Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus speaks them. Any lesion on this loop spoils repetition. The transcortical aphasias come from damage in the border (watershed) zones around the loop, which cut it off from the rest of the cortex but leave it intact, so repetition survives. In transcortical motor aphasia the lesion lies in front of or above Broca's area, often in the supplementary motor area or a watershed infarct between the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. Spontaneous speech is sparse and effortful, with long delays, but comprehension is fair and repetition is good. Arjun, after a stroke of this kind, barely starts a sentence on his own, yet repeats 'the train leaves Chennai at nine' word for word. Transcortical sensory aphasia is the posterior mirror image: fluent speech, poor comprehension, preserved repetition, often with echolalia. Option c is right because the perisylvian loop is spared, so the patient can echo words he cannot produce spontaneously. Exam trap: conduction aphasia sounds like a 'repetition' answer, but it is the aphasia defined by very poor repetition with good comprehension.", "topic": "Transcortical motor aphasia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In the construction of a psychological test, for the sharpest discrimination among examinees, the item index difficulty should be set at:", "options": {"a": "40%", "b": "50%", "c": "60%", "d": "80%"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: an item difficulty of 50% gives the sharpest discrimination among examinees. The item difficulty index (p) is simply the proportion of examinees who pass an item, so a high p means an easy item: 80% passing is easy, 40% passing is hard. An item passed by everyone or by no one tells nobody apart and adds nothing to the test. Each item discriminates by separating those who pass it from those who fail it. With 100 examinees, an item passed by 50 separates 50 times 50, or 2,500 pairs of people; one passed by 80 separates only 80 times 20, or 1,600 pairs. Statistically, the variance of a pass-fail item is p times q (q is 1 minus p), and it peaks at p = 0.50, where it equals 0.25. Because the discrimination index cannot be high when p is near 0 or 1, items near 0.50 also allow the highest discrimination. So when Meera builds a 40-item aptitude test, she keeps items whose difficulty clusters around 0.50, perhaps spread from 0.30 to 0.70 so the whole ability range is covered. Guessing shifts the ideal a little: for multiple-choice items the best p lies midway between the chance score and 1.00, about 0.62 for four options. Mastery and screening tests deliberately use easier items, since they only need to pick out a small failing group. Option b is right because a 50% pass rate maximises item variance and the number of pass-fail distinctions. Exam trap: examiners count on confusing the index with hardness; a 'high' difficulty index such as 80% means an easy item, not a hard one.", "topic": "Optimal item difficulty", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A lesion at the optic chiasma in the visual pathway results in:", "options": {"a": "Cortical blindness", "b": "Bitemporal hemianopia", "c": "Homonymous hemianopia", "d": "Quadrantanopia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: a lesion at the optic chiasm causes bitemporal hemianopia, loss of the outer (temporal) half of the visual field in each eye. Each retina has a nasal half (towards the nose) and a temporal half (towards the temple). Because the lens inverts the image, the nasal half-retina receives light from the temporal (outer) visual field. At the optic chiasm, just below the hypothalamus and above the pituitary gland, fibres from the two nasal half-retinas cross to the opposite side, while temporal fibres stay on their own side. This partial crossing sends the whole left visual field to the right hemisphere and the right field to the left. A lesion pressing on the middle of the chiasm cuts only the crossing nasal fibres, so both temporal fields are lost and the patient keeps a central, tunnel-like band of vision. The classic cause is a pituitary adenoma growing upwards. Kabir, a 45-year-old clerk, keeps bumping into people on both sides and has stopped driving because he cannot see cars beside him; an MRI shows a pituitary tumour. Lesions behind the chiasm (optic tract, optic radiations or primary visual cortex) give a homonymous hemianopia, the same half of the field lost in both eyes, while a lesion of one optic nerve blinds one eye. Option b is right because the chiasm is the one place where the fibres serving both temporal fields come together. Exam trap: remember 'nasal fibres cross, temporal fields are lost'; mixing up retinal halves with field halves leads students to nasal or homonymous losses.", "topic": "Optic chiasm and field defects", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Narcolepsy is characterised by: 1. Hypnagogic hallucinations 2. Repeated irresistible attacks of refreshing sleep 3. Cataplexy 4. Prolonged, deep nocturnal sleep. Choose the correct option:", "options": {"a": "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "b": "1, 2 and 4 are correct", "c": "2, 3 and 4 are correct", "d": "1, 3 and 4 are correct"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: narcolepsy is characterised by hypnagogic hallucinations, repeated irresistible attacks of refreshing sleep, and cataplexy, so 1, 2 and 3 are correct. Narcolepsy, named by Jean-Baptiste Gelineau (1880), is a disorder in which REM sleep intrudes into waking. Its classic tetrad is excessive daytime sleepiness with sleep attacks, cataplexy, hypnagogic (falling asleep) or hypnopompic (waking) hallucinations, and sleep paralysis. Sleep attacks are irresistible, often brief, and leave the person refreshed for a while. Cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion, especially laughter, with full consciousness: REM atonia breaking into wakefulness. The hallucinations and sleep paralysis are REM dream imagery and REM paralysis at the edges of sleep. Recordings show sleep-onset REM periods, REM within minutes of falling asleep. The cause of type 1 narcolepsy is loss of the orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus: Emmanuel Mignot and colleagues (1999) traced narcolepsy in dogs to a faulty hypocretin receptor gene, and in 2000 his group and others showed that human patients have lost these neurons. Riya, a college student, falls asleep in every afternoon lecture, wakes from a 15 minute nap feeling clear, and her knees buckle when her friends make her laugh. Statement 4 is false. Night sleep in narcolepsy is not prolonged and deep; it is fragmented, with frequent awakenings and vivid dreams. Total sleep over 24 hours is roughly normal; it is simply badly placed. Option a is right because it holds the three true features and leaves out the false one. Exam trap: long, deep night sleep with unrefreshing naps and 'sleep drunkenness' on waking describes idiopathic hypersomnia, the look-alike borrowed for statement 4.", "topic": "Narcolepsy tetrad", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A Posterior pituitary hormone:", "options": {"a": "Prolactin", "b": "Somatostatin", "c": "Oxytocin", "d": "Thyrotropin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone. The pituitary has two parts with different origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is true glandular tissue. It makes its own hormones, growth hormone, prolactin, thyroid-stimulating hormone (thyrotropin), adrenocorticotropic hormone and the gonadotropins FSH and LH, under the control of releasing and inhibiting hormones that the hypothalamus sends down a local portal blood system. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is neural tissue, an outgrowth of the hypothalamus, and makes nothing itself. Oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) are synthesised in neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, carried down their axons, stored in the posterior pituitary and released into the blood. Oxytocin contracts the uterus in labour and squeezes milk out of the breast during suckling (the let-down reflex); it also shapes bonding and trust. Vasopressin makes the kidneys retain water. When Meera nurses her newborn, prolactin keeps her milk supply going, while each feed triggers an oxytocin surge that lets the milk flow. Option c is right because oxytocin is one of only two hormones released from the posterior lobe. Exam trap: prolactin and oxytocin both concern breastfeeding, so they are swapped; prolactin (anterior pituitary) makes milk, oxytocin (posterior pituitary) ejects it. A second trap is thyrotropin versus thyrotropin-releasing hormone: TSH comes from the anterior pituitary, TRH from the hypothalamus.", "topic": "Posterior pituitary hormones", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The non-parametric equivalent of the ANOVA test is:", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Run test", "c": "Kruskal-Wallis H test", "d": "Wilcoxon Signed rank test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Kruskal-Wallis H test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA. Parametric tests such as the t test and ANOVA compare means and assume interval or ratio scores, roughly normal distributions and equal variances. When those assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or badly skewed scores, researchers turn to non-parametric (distribution-free) tests, which work on ranks. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) extended the rank-sum logic of the Mann-Whitney U test to three or more independent groups. All scores from all groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest; the mean rank of each group is compared with the overall mean rank, and the resulting H statistic is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom (k is the number of groups). A significant H, like a significant F, says only that at least one group differs, so pairwise follow-up tests are needed. Suppose Meera compares pain ratings on a 1 to 5 scale from three groups of patients given yoga, physiotherapy or medication alone. The ratings are ordinal and skewed, so instead of a one-way ANOVA she runs a Kruskal-Wallis test. Learn the whole matching table: independent t test to Mann-Whitney U; paired t test to Wilcoxon signed-rank; one-way ANOVA to Kruskal-Wallis H; repeated-measures ANOVA to the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937); Pearson's r to Spearman's rho or Kendall's tau. Option c is right because Kruskal-Wallis, like one-way ANOVA, compares three or more independent groups, using ranks instead of means. Exam trap: Mann-Whitney U is the best-known rank test and is literally the two-group case of Kruskal-Wallis, so it tempts, but its parametric partner is the independent t test.", "topic": "Kruskal-Wallis H test", "fig": "William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis"}, {"q": "Match List I (Disorder) with List II (Symptoms): a. Generalized Anxiety disorder, b. Dementia, c. Schizophrenia, d. Complex partial. 1. Insidious or sudden onset, 2. Deja vu, 3. Elevated blink rate, 4. Persistent worry", "options": {"a": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2", "b": "a-4, b-1, c-3, d-2", "c": "a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4", "d": "a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a-4, b-1, c-3, d-2, pairing generalised anxiety disorder with persistent worry, dementia with insidious or sudden onset, schizophrenia with an elevated blink rate, and complex partial seizures with deja vu. Generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is defined by persistent, excessive worry about many everyday matters that is hard to control, on more days than not for at least 6 months in DSM-5, with restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension or poor sleep. Dementia is an acquired decline in memory and other cognitive abilities that disrupts daily life, and its onset depends on the cause. Alzheimer's disease begins insidiously and progresses gradually, whereas vascular dementia often starts suddenly after a stroke and worsens stepwise, so 'insidious or sudden onset' covers the dementias as a group. In schizophrenia, the spontaneous eye blink rate is raised in many unmedicated patients; because blinking rises with central dopamine activity, studies from the late 1970s onward read it as a sign of dopamine overactivity, and it tends to fall with antipsychotic treatment. Complex partial seizures (now called focal impaired awareness seizures) usually arise in the temporal lobe. They often begin with an aura such as deja vu, the feeling that a new moment has been lived before, or a rising feeling in the stomach; then the person stares, loses awareness and shows automatisms such as lip smacking. Kabir, 30, suddenly feels he has lived this exact moment before, then stares blankly and fumbles with his shirt for a minute, remembering nothing afterwards. Option b is right because it is the only option that gets all four pairs. Exam trap: deja vu tempts as an odd experience for schizophrenia, but as a brief, stereotyped aura it points to temporal lobe epilepsy.", "topic": "Matching disorders to signs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The fractional antedating goal response proposed by Hull is roughly equivalent to:", "options": {"a": "Expectancy", "b": "Trial and Error", "c": "Insight", "d": "Solution learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Clark Hull's fractional antedating (anticipatory) goal response is roughly equivalent to expectancy. Clark Hull (1943) built a mechanistic stimulus-response theory of learning on drive reduction, set out in Principles of Behavior. He still had to explain behaviour that looks purposeful, as when a rat runs eagerly toward a goal box it cannot yet see. In papers of 1930 and 1931 he proposed the fractional antedating goal response, written rg. When a hungry rat eats in the goal box, the full goal response (chewing, salivating, licking) is conditioned to the cues there. Because cues in the alley resemble goal box cues, small fragments of the goal response, such as tiny chewing or licking movements, become conditioned to them and move forward in time, occurring before the goal is reached. Each fragment produces its own internal stimulus, sg, and this rg-sg mechanism links the chain of responses and keeps the animal directed toward the goal. In effect the rat anticipates food without Hull having to speak of mental states. Arjun, walking to the hostel canteen at lunchtime, starts salivating and speeds up before he sees the food; in Hull's terms, his rg-sg is pulling him along. Edward Tolman (1932) explained the same purposive behaviour with expectancies, cognitive beliefs that a response will lead to a goal. Hull's rg is often called his stimulus-response translation of Tolman's expectancy. Option a is right because rg is Hull's mechanistic stand-in for an animal's expectation of the goal. Exam trap: 'fractional' sounds like part-learning or step-by-step learning, so trial and error is chosen, but rg concerns anticipation of the goal, not how a response is first found.", "topic": "Hull's fractional goal response", "fig": "Clark Hull"}, {"q": "The interactive and independent effects of two or more independent variables on the dependent variable is studied using:", "options": {"a": "Within groups design", "b": "Factorial design", "c": "Matched group design", "d": "Pre-experimental design"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a factorial design studies both the independent (main) effects and the interactive effects of two or more independent variables on a dependent variable. In a factorial design every level of each independent variable (factor) is combined with every level of the others, so the conditions form a grid of cells. Ronald Fisher (1935) promoted the approach in The Design of Experiments, arguing that varying several factors together is more efficient and more informative than studying one factor at a time. A design with two factors of two levels each is called a 2 x 2 design and has four cells; a 2 x 3 design has six. The analysis, usually a factorial ANOVA, gives a main effect for each factor, the effect of that factor averaged over the levels of the others, and an interaction, which exists when the effect of one factor depends on the level of another. Suppose Kabir studies exam performance with two factors: type of revision (spaced or massed) and anxiety level (high or low). He might find a main effect of revision (spaced is better overall), a main effect of anxiety, and an interaction: spacing helps the low-anxiety students much more than the high-anxiety ones. Plotted, the lines for the two anxiety groups are not parallel, the visual sign of an interaction. Only a factorial arrangement can reveal such a dependency. Option b is right because the factorial design is the one built to estimate the independent and interactive effects of several independent variables together. Exam trap: within-groups and matched-groups designs describe how participants are assigned, not how many factors are crossed; a factorial design can itself be between, within or mixed.", "topic": "Factorial design and interactions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which theory of emotion contends that arousal and appraisal are independent processes brought about by the action of the thalamus?", "options": {"a": "James-Lange theory", "b": "Cannon-Bard Theory", "c": "Schachter-Singer theory", "d": "Activation theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Cannon-Bard theory holds that arousal and the experience of emotion are independent, simultaneous processes triggered by the thalamus. Walter Cannon (1927) and his student Philip Bard (1928) attacked the earlier James-Lange theory, which said that bodily arousal comes first and the felt emotion is our perception of it (we are afraid because we tremble). Cannon raised several objections: animals whose viscera were surgically cut off from the brain still showed emotional behaviour; the same visceral changes occur in fear, anger, fever and exercise, so they cannot tell emotions apart; the viscera respond too slowly to explain the speed of emotion; and injecting adrenaline produces arousal without a true emotion. In the Cannon-Bard view, an emotional stimulus is relayed through the thalamus, which sends signals at the same time upward to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling (the 'appraisal' of the stem), and downward to the body through the hypothalamus and autonomic nervous system, producing arousal. Neither causes the other. When Riya sees a snake on the path to her hostel, she feels fear and her heart races at the same instant, each from the thalamic signal. Bard's work on decorticate cats showed that 'sham rage' depended on the hypothalamus, which later shifted the centre downward, but the textbook theory still names the thalamus. Option b is right because only Cannon-Bard makes feeling and arousal independent and simultaneous via the thalamus. Exam trap: the Schachter-Singer theory also speaks of arousal and appraisal, but it makes them interact, with a cognitive label interpreting the arousal, rather than run independently.", "topic": "Cannon-Bard theory of emotion", "fig": "Walter Cannon and Philip Bard"}, {"q": "An amnesic agent used in memory consolidation studies that produces a condition known as spreading depression:", "options": {"a": "Acetoxycycloheximide", "b": "Potassium Chloride", "c": "Puromycin", "d": "Electroconvulsive shock (ECS)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: potassium chloride (KCl) applied to the cortex produces spreading depression and was used as an amnesic agent in memory consolidation studies. The consolidation hypothesis of Georg Elias Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) says a new memory stays fragile for a while before it becomes stable. Researchers tested it by giving an amnesic agent at different times after training: if a treatment given soon after learning wipes out the memory but the same treatment given hours later does not, the memory must have been consolidating in between. Several agents were used. Carl Duncan (1949) gave rats electroconvulsive shock and found such a retrograde gradient. Protein synthesis inhibitors such as puromycin and acetoxycycloheximide blocked the formation of long-term memory. Spreading depression works differently. Aristides Leao (1944) discovered spreading depression: a strong local stimulus (in later work usually concentrated KCl placed on the exposed cortex) sets off a wave of depolarisation that creeps across the cortex at a few millimetres per minute, silencing neural activity and flattening the EEG for minutes to hours. The cortex then recovers, so the method gives a reversible 'functional ablation'. Jan Bures and Olga Buresova (1960s) used it to depress one hemisphere of a rat during training or testing, showing that a memory could be confined to one side and studying how it transferred between hemispheres. Option b is right because KCl is the classic agent that produces cortical spreading depression. Exam trap: all four options disrupt memory, so the clue is the named condition: spreading depression means KCl, protein synthesis inhibition means puromycin or acetoxycycloheximide, and a seizure means ECS.", "topic": "Spreading depression and consolidation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Suppose the reliability of an intelligence test is 0.40. How many times should the test be lengthened to reach a reliability coefficient of 0.80?", "options": {"a": "4", "b": "3", "c": "2", "d": "6"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the test must be made 6 times as long to raise its reliability from 0.40 to 0.80. Reliability rises with test length because random errors in individual items tend to cancel out as more items are added, while the true score part keeps adding up. Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910) independently published the formula that predicts this, now called the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula. In its general form, the new reliability equals k times r divided by [1 plus (k minus 1) times r], where r is the old reliability and k is the factor by which the length changes. Solving for k gives k = r_new (1 minus r_old) divided by [r_old (1 minus r_new)]. Here r_old is 0.40 and r_new is 0.80. The numerator is 0.80 x 0.60 = 0.48; the denominator is 0.40 x 0.20 = 0.08; so k = 0.48 / 0.08 = 6. Check by putting 6 back in: 6 x 0.40 = 2.4, and 1 + 5 x 0.40 = 3.0, so 2.4 / 3.0 = 0.80. If the original test had 20 items, it would need 120 comparable items. The formula assumes the added items are parallel to the old ones, measuring the same thing with similar difficulty and quality; adding poor or off-target items will not deliver the predicted gain. It also shows diminishing returns: each extra block of items adds less. Option d is right because only a sixfold lengthening brings 0.40 up to exactly 0.80. Exam trap: it feels natural that doubling a reliability needs a doubled test, but doubling gives only 0.80 / 1.40 = 0.57; tripling gives 0.67 and quadrupling 0.73.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown prophecy formula", "fig": "Charles Spearman and William Brown"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "options": {"a": "Drug Abuse Resistance Education (DARE)", "b": "Genetic Screening", "c": "Narcotics Anonymous (NA)", "d": "Prenatal health care"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention; it serves people who already have an addiction, so it is tertiary prevention. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, adapted the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention aims to stop a disorder from arising at all, lowering its incidence (new cases) through education, stress reduction, prenatal care, nutrition and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention aims at early detection and prompt treatment of cases that have already begun, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention aims to reduce the disability and relapse of people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention and rehabilitation. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Genetic screening and counselling identify carriers or risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Prenatal care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Arjun, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it largely ineffective, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "topic": "Levels of prevention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All of the following are true for multiple correlation coefficient R1.23 in a trivariate distribution EXCEPT:", "options": {"a": "0 < R1.23 < 1", "b": "If R1.23 = 0, then all total and partial correlations involving X1 are zero", "c": "-1 ≤ R1.23 ≤ 1", "d": "If R1.23 = 1, the association is perfect, and all the regression residues are zero"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the statement that R1.23 ranges from minus 1 to plus 1 is false, because a multiple correlation coefficient is never negative. In a trivariate distribution, R1.23 is the multiple correlation between one variable, X1, and the best linear combination of the other two, X2 and X3. Least-squares regression finds the weights that make the predicted X1 values as close as possible to the actual ones, and R1.23 is simply the ordinary correlation between actual and predicted X1. Because the weights are chosen to maximise this correlation, any negative relation is absorbed into a negative weight, so R1.23 runs from 0 to 1. Its square is the proportion of variance in X1 explained by X2 and X3 together, and R1.23 is never smaller than r12 or r13 taken alone (ignoring sign). Suppose Meera predicts students' exam marks (X1) from study hours (X2) and sleep (X3) and gets R1.23 = 0.60: the two together explain 36% of the variance in marks. The boundary cases are statements b and d. If R1.23 = 0, X1 is uncorrelated with both X2 and X3, so r12, r13 and the partial correlations r12.3 and r13.2 are all zero. If R1.23 = 1, every point lies exactly on the regression plane and all residuals are zero. Option c is right because it gives R1.23 a negative range it cannot take. Exam trap: the range of Pearson's r (minus 1 to 1) is carried over by habit, but R measures only the size of an association, not its direction. Key note: option a is printed with strict signs (0 < R1.23 < 1), and strictly R can equal 0 or 1, as options b and d themselves discuss; a is the textbook '0 to 1' range with the equals signs lost in print, and the false statement the setter wants is c, so c stands.", "topic": "Range of multiple correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The only memory scale that can be used with both children and adults:", "options": {"a": "Fuld Object Memory Evaluation", "b": "Wide Range Assessment of Memory and Learning (WRAML-2)", "c": "Rivermead Behavioural Memory Test", "d": "Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test (RAVLT)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Wide Range Assessment of Memory and Learning, Second Edition (WRAML-2) is the memory battery normed for both children and adults. David Sheslow and Wayne Adams first published the WRAML in 1990 for ages 5 to 17. The second edition (2003) extended the norms to ages 5 to 90, so a single battery covers childhood through old age. Its core subtests pair into indices: Story Memory and Verbal Learning give a Verbal Memory Index, Design Memory and Picture Memory a Visual Memory Index, and Finger Windows and Number Letter an Attention/Concentration Index, which combine into a General Memory Index. Because the same tasks and score metric run across the whole age range, a clinician can compare memory profiles at any age on one scale. Kabir, aged 10, referred for a learning difficulty, and his grandmother, aged 70, referred after a mild stroke, could both be assessed with the WRAML-2. The other options were built for narrower groups. The Fuld Object Memory Evaluation (Paula Fuld, 1977) has older adults identify ten common objects by touch and recall them, to screen for dementia. The Rivermead Behavioural Memory Test (Barbara Wilson, Janet Cockburn and Alan Baddeley, 1985) assesses everyday memory, such as remembering a name, an appointment or a route, in adults with brain injury. The Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test (Andre Rey, 1941) is a 15-word list-learning test used mainly with adults. Option b is right because WRAML-2 was designed and normed to span children and adults in one instrument. Exam trap: the RAVLT is so widely used that it tempts, but it is a single list-learning test, not a child-and-adult battery. Key note: RAVLT and Rivermead have child adaptations or norms, so 'the only' is loose; WRAML-2 is the one battery built and normed for both in one test, so b stands.", "topic": "Memory tests across the lifespan", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The 'threshold method' of breaking a habit proposed by Guthrie is very much like:", "options": {"a": "Assertiveness training", "b": "Extinction", "c": "Systematic desensitisation", "d": "Chaining"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Edwin Guthrie's (1935) threshold method of breaking a habit is very much like systematic desensitisation. In The Psychology of Learning, Guthrie held a pure contiguity theory: a combination of stimuli that accompanied a movement will, when it recurs, tend to be followed by that movement, and the bond forms at full strength in a single trial. If a habit is just a cue tied to a response, it is broken by getting the cue to occur while some other response happens, so the new response becomes attached to it. Guthrie described three ways. In the threshold method the cue is introduced so faintly that it does not set off the unwanted response, then raised in small steps, always kept below the level that triggers it; a horse learns to accept a saddle when a light blanket is laid on its back first, then heavier blankets, then the saddle. In the fatigue method the response is provoked again and again until the animal is exhausted and stops. In the incompatible response method the cue is presented while a response that cannot occur together with the habit is going on. Option c is right because Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation works the same way: the client climbs a graded hierarchy of feared situations, from the least to the most frightening, each step kept weak enough that relaxation, not anxiety, wins. Meera, afraid of dogs, first looks at a photo of a puppy while relaxed, then watches a dog across the street, and only later pats one. Exam trap: desensitisation also pairs the cue with relaxation, an incompatible response (Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition), so students match it only to the incompatible response method; its graded, step-by-step hierarchy is what makes it the direct descendant of the threshold method.", "topic": "Guthrie's habit-breaking methods", "fig": "Edwin Guthrie"}, {"q": "The reinforcement centre in the brain:", "options": {"a": "Basal nucleus", "b": "Basolateral nucleus", "c": "Central nucleus", "d": "Nucleus Accumbens"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the nucleus accumbens is the brain's best-known reinforcement (reward) centre. It lies in the ventral striatum at the base of the forebrain, where the caudate nucleus and putamen join, and it receives the mesolimbic dopamine pathway from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain. The story began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), who found that rats with electrodes in the septal area would press a lever again and again, for hours, just to stimulate their own brains. Later mapping showed that the most rewarding sites lie along the medial forebrain bundle, which carries VTA dopamine fibres to the accumbens. Natural rewards such as food and sex, and almost every addictive drug, raise dopamine in the accumbens: cocaine and amphetamine act directly on dopamine terminals there, while opioids, nicotine and alcohol work more indirectly through the VTA. Dopamine here signals wanting and the prediction of reward more than pleasure itself. When Kabir hears his phone ping and feels the pull to check it at once, accumbens dopamine is part of that pull. Option d is right because the nucleus accumbens is the core of the brain's reinforcement circuit, the site where brain self-stimulation, natural rewards and drug reward meet. Exam trap: the options look like a matched set because each is a 'nucleus', but the basolateral and central nuclei belong to the amygdala (fear learning and fear output), and the basal nucleus of Meynert is the cholinergic cell group lost in Alzheimer's disease; none of them is the reward centre.", "topic": "Reward centre of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The word 'currency' is appropriate in the context of:", "options": {"a": "Oral Personalities", "b": "Anal Personalities", "c": "Phallic Personalities", "d": "Genital Personalities"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the word 'currency' belongs to the anal personality. In Sigmund Freud's (1905) psychosexual theory, the anal stage runs from about 18 months to 3 years, when pleasure centres on holding back and expelling faeces and toilet training is the first big clash between an instinct and social demands. In 'Character and Anal Erotism' (1908) Freud described the adult anal character by a triad of traits: orderly, parsimonious (careful or stingy with money) and obstinate. He argued that the child's interest in faeces is later displaced on to money, so money, gold and currency become symbols of the stool the child once held back or handed over as a 'gift'. He pointed to old myths and folk sayings that link gold with dirt and excrement as support. A child who fights toilet training by holding back may become anal-retentive: neat, punctual, miserly, a hoarder of things and of money. A child who fights by soiling may become anal-expulsive: messy, wasteful and careless with money. Arjun, who counts every rupee, files every receipt in labelled folders and will not lend money to friends, fits the anal-retentive picture. Option b is right because Freud tied money (currency) directly to the anal stage through the symbolic link between faeces and money. Exam trap: 'currency' sounds like greed or getting things, which students link to the oral stage's 'taking in'; but oral traits concern dependence, eating, smoking and talking, while saving, hoarding and stinginess with money are the anal mark.", "topic": "Anal character and money", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "If the mean IQ of Indians is 100 with a standard deviation of 16, what percentage of Indians would you expect to score at or below 132 on an IQ test?", "options": {"a": "34%", "b": "68%", "c": "98%", "d": "84%"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: about 98% of people would score at or below 132. To read a score on a normal curve, first turn it into a z score, the number of standard deviations it lies from the mean: z = (X minus mean) / SD = (132 minus 100) / 16 = 32 / 16 = +2.0. Then use the known areas of the normal curve. About 34.1% of cases lie between the mean and 1 SD on each side, about 13.6% between 1 and 2 SD, and about 2.1% between 2 and 3 SD. Half the curve, 50%, lies below the mean. So the area at or below z = +2 is 50 + 34.1 + 13.6 = 97.7%, which rounds to 98%. Put another way, an IQ of 132 on this scale sits at about the 98th percentile, and only about 2% of people score higher. Option c is right because 132 lies exactly two standard deviations above the mean, and about 98% of a normal distribution falls at or below +2 SD. The SD of 16 is not a misprint: it was the convention of the Stanford-Binet deviation IQ from 1960 until the fifth edition (2003), while the Wechsler scales use 15; with an SD of 15 the same score of 132 would give z of about 2.13, a little higher. Exam trap: each wrong option is a real normal-curve figure attached to a different question. 84% is the area at or below +1 SD (an IQ of 116 here); 68% is the area within 1 SD on both sides (84 to 116); 34% is the area from the mean to +1 SD. Always check whether a question asks for the area below a score, between two scores or within a band around the mean.", "topic": "Normal curve and IQ percentiles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A newly constructed test of creativity has a +0.15 correlation with four other standardised creativity scales and a correlation of +1.00 between the scores of any student who takes the test twice. This indicates that the test has:", "options": {"a": "High content validity but low reliability", "b": "Moderate external validity and moderate reliability", "c": "Low face validity but high reliability", "d": "Low criterion validity but high reliability"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the test has low criterion validity but high reliability. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same result each time? A test-retest correlation of +1.00 means every student got the same score both times, which is perfect reliability (stability). Validity is accuracy: does the test measure what it claims to measure? One way to check is criterion-related validity, correlating the new test with an outside standard. When the standard is a set of established tests of the same trait given at about the same time, the result is concurrent validity, and in the multitrait-multimethod terms of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) it is also evidence of convergent validity. Here the new test correlates only +0.15 with four standard creativity scales, so it shares very little with them: whatever it measures so consistently, it is probably not creativity as those scales define it. Option d is right because a near-zero correlation with accepted criterion measures means low criterion validity, while the perfect retest correlation means high reliability. The case shows the classic rule that reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. A bathroom scale that always reads 5 kg too light is perfectly reliable but not valid. Exam trap: students see 'high reliability' and jump to option c, but face validity is judged simply by looking at the items (does it look like a creativity test to those who take it?), so no correlation can show it is low. The same holds for content validity in option a, which experts judge by inspecting how well the items cover the domain; and a's 'low reliability' is wrong in any case.", "topic": "Reliability without validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All of the following are characteristic features of conversion disorder, EXCEPT:", "options": {"a": "Symptoms affect voluntary motor or sensory functions", "b": "La belle indifference", "c": "Non-volitional aetiology", "d": "Gradual, planned response to conflict"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a gradual, planned response to conflict is not a feature of conversion disorder. Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5, is the old 'hysteria': a person loses or changes a voluntary motor or sensory function (weakness or paralysis of a limb, tremor, non-epileptic seizures, blindness, numbness, loss of voice) although no neurological disease explains it. Examination finds signs that do not fit anatomy, such as glove anaesthesia that stops sharply at the wrist or Hoover's sign in a 'paralysed' leg. The classic textbook features are these: the symptoms affect voluntary motor or sensory functions; they are not intentionally produced (non-volitional), so the person is not faking; onset is often sudden, soon after a stressor or conflict; and some patients show la belle indifference, a calm unconcern about a dramatic disability, a phrase usually credited to Pierre Janet (1859 to 1947). Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) saw the symptom as an unconscious conflict 'converted' into a bodily one, bringing primary gain (the conflict stays out of awareness) and secondary gain (sympathy, escape from duties). When Ananya suddenly cannot move her right arm on the morning of a board exam she dreads, and no nerve or brain damage is found, that is the picture. Option d is right because conversion is an unconscious, usually abrupt reaction, while a gradual, planned, deliberate response points to factitious disorder (to take on the sick role) or malingering (for external gain). Exam trap: DSM-5 warns that la belle indifference should not be used to make the diagnosis, because it is not specific and also occurs in real neurological illness, yet textbooks still list it as a characteristic feature, so option b is a feature and not the exception.", "topic": "Conversion disorder features", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Jellinek's typology of alcoholism, deliberate, undisciplined drinking is a characteristic of:", "options": {"a": "Alpha alcoholics", "b": "Beta alcoholics", "c": "Gamma alcoholics", "d": "Delta alcoholics"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Elvin Morton Jellinek's (1960) typology, deliberate, undisciplined drinking marks alpha alcoholics. In The Disease Concept of Alcoholism, Jellinek sorted problem drinkers into five 'species' named with Greek letters. Alpha alcoholism is purely psychological dependence: the person drinks deliberately to relieve bodily or emotional pain, worry or tension. The drinking is 'undisciplined' in that it breaks the social rules about when, where and how much people drink, and it can strain family and work, but there is no loss of control, no inability to abstain and no withdrawal, and it need not get worse. Beta alcoholism brings physical complications such as cirrhosis, gastritis or nerve damage from heavy drinking, without physical or psychological dependence. Gamma alcoholism, the type most common in Anglo-American countries and in Alcoholics Anonymous, shows tolerance, withdrawal, craving and loss of control: once started, the person cannot stop. Delta alcoholism, common in wine cultures such as France, shows inability to abstain even for a day, though the amount at each sitting stays controlled. Epsilon alcoholism is periodic or binge drinking. Jellinek counted only gamma and delta as true diseases. Ravi, who reaches for whisky every evening to calm his work anxiety and drinks more than his circle thinks proper, yet can stop when he chooses and has no withdrawal, fits alpha. Option a is right because alpha alcoholism is defined by deliberate, relief-seeking drinking that breaks social discipline without loss of control. Exam trap: 'loss of control' (gamma) and 'inability to abstain' (delta) are the two most often swapped; the word 'deliberate' signals that control is intact, which rules out both.", "topic": "Jellinek's types of alcoholism", "fig": "Elvin Morton Jellinek"}, {"q": "A post-reinforcement pause is a salient feature of which of the following schedule of reinforcement?", "options": {"a": "Fixed Interval, Variable Ratio", "b": "Variable Ratio, Variable Ratio", "c": "Fixed Interval, Fixed Ratio", "d": "Variable Interval, Fixed Ratio"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the post-reinforcement pause is a salient feature of fixed interval and fixed ratio schedules. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, mapped four basic partial schedules by crossing ratio (reward after a number of responses) with interval (reward for the first response after a period of time), each either fixed or variable. A post-reinforcement pause is a break in responding just after each reinforcer. It appears when the schedule is fixed, because the animal can in effect tell that the next reward is not available yet. On a fixed interval schedule the pause is followed by a gradual speeding up as the time for the next reward nears, giving the scalloped curve on a cumulative record. On a fixed ratio schedule the animal pauses, then runs off the whole ratio at a high, steady rate, the break-and-run pattern; the pause grows longer as the ratio gets larger. On variable schedules the next reward could follow the very next response or come at the next moment, so there is no safe time to rest, and responding is steady with little or no pausing. Priya, paid a fixed amount for every 50 envelopes she packs, takes a short break after each batch before starting the next: a fixed ratio pause. Option c is right because both fixed schedules, and only they, produce a reliable pause after reinforcement. Exam trap: every wrong option contains a variable schedule, and variable ratio, which appears in two of them (twice in b), is the schedule famous for the highest, steadiest rate with almost no pausing.", "topic": "Post-reinforcement pause", "fig": "Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "The neural basis for non-associative learning (viz., habituation and sensitisation) can be explained by:", "options": {"a": "Long-term potentiation", "b": "Facilitation", "c": "Generalised imitation", "d": "Equipotentiality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the neural basis of habituation and sensitisation is explained by changes in synaptic facilitation. Non-associative learning is a change in the response to a single kind of stimulus, without pairing it with anything else. In habituation the response to a repeated harmless stimulus weakens; in sensitisation a strong or noxious stimulus makes responses to many other stimuli stronger. Eric Kandel (from the 1960s; Nobel Prize 2000) traced both to identified synapses in the sea slug Aplysia. A light touch to its siphon makes it withdraw its gill. With repeated touches the sensory neurons release less glutamate onto the motor neurons (homosynaptic depression, the reverse of facilitation), so the reflex habituates. A shock to the tail activates facilitating interneurons that release serotonin onto the sensory neuron terminals; serotonin raises cyclic AMP and protein kinase A, prolongs the action potential and increases transmitter release. This presynaptic facilitation makes the gill withdrawal stronger: sensitisation. The strength of one synapse is thus turned down or up, and facilitation is the textbook name for the mechanism. Sneha stops noticing the ceiling fan after a few minutes (habituation) but jumps at every small sound after a near-accident on the road (sensitisation). Option b is right because non-associative learning in Aplysia rests on presynaptic facilitation (and its mirror image, synaptic depression) at the sensory-to-motor synapse, not on new links between stimuli. Exam trap: long-term potentiation is also a change in synaptic strength, so it tempts students, but it was found in the hippocampus by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) and is the model for associative learning, because it depends on the pre- and postsynaptic cells being active together.", "topic": "Neural basis of non-associative learning", "fig": "Eric Kandel"}, {"q": "Allport's concept of functional autonomy resembles the Freudian notion of:", "options": {"a": "Intellectualization", "b": "Introjection", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Reaction formation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Gordon Allport's (1937) functional autonomy resembles the Freudian notion of sublimation. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, Allport argued that adult motives can become independent of the needs that first started them. A sailor first goes to sea to earn a living; years later, rich and retired, he still longs for the sea for its own sake. Allport later (1961) split this into perseverative functional autonomy (low-level habits and routines that persist) and propriate functional autonomy (motives bound up with the self and its values). Sublimation, for Sigmund Freud (1905), is the healthiest way of handling an impulse, often called the most mature defence: an unacceptable sexual or aggressive impulse is redirected into a socially valued activity such as art, science, sport or surgery. Option c is right because in both ideas a mature, valued adult activity looks quite unlike the need it grew out of, and in both the present motive is a transformed descendant of an older one. The difference is the fuel. Freud held that the sublimated activity is still driven by the original libidinal or aggressive energy, now disguised; Allport held that the new motive runs on its own and no longer needs its root. Rohan, who began learning the tabla to please his father and now practises at midnight because he loves it, illustrates Allport. Exam trap: knowing that Allport rejected Freud's account of adult motives, students think no Freudian idea can resemble functional autonomy, but the question asks only which one looks most alike. Key note: under the options the paper prints a stray line, 'motives or desires and the current expressed behaviors or attitudes', the tail of a stem about a break between original motives and current behaviour; reaction formation also shows such a break, but it turns a desire into its opposite and keeps the original desire active and hidden, which is far from functional autonomy; c stands.", "topic": "Functional autonomy and sublimation", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Kandel's studies of the sea slug aplysia were used to support the hypothesis that:", "options": {"a": "Even simple animals like sea slugs are capable of insight learning", "b": "Basic changes in neural pathways are heritable", "c": "Basic changes in neural pathways occur with changes in learning and memory", "d": "Chemical cues are used in proprioception"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: Eric Kandel's studies of Aplysia (from the 1960s onwards; Nobel Prize 2000, shared with Arvid Carlsson and Paul Greengard) supported the hypothesis that basic changes in neural pathways occur with learning and memory. Kandel chose the sea slug Aplysia because its nervous system has only about 20,000 neurons, many large enough to be identified and recorded one by one in every animal. He studied the gill-withdrawal reflex: touch the siphon and the gill pulls in. He showed that habituation, sensitisation and even classical conditioning of this reflex are stored as changes in the strength of specific synapses between sensory neurons, interneurons and motor neurons. Short-term memory, lasting minutes, uses changes in existing proteins: serotonin raises cyclic AMP and protein kinase A, which increases transmitter release. Long-term memory, lasting days, needs new gene expression: repeated training sends protein kinase A into the nucleus, where it switches on CREB, new proteins are made, and the sensory neuron grows new synaptic connections (or loses some, in long-term habituation). This was some of the first direct cellular evidence that learning physically changes the wiring of a nervous system, an idea earlier proposed by Santiago Ramon y Cajal (1894) and Donald Hebb (1949). Option c is right because Kandel's whole programme showed that learning and memory are carried by changes in specific neural pathways. When Anjali practises a raga daily for months, the same principle applies: the synapses she uses grow stronger and more numerous. Exam trap: 'heritable' (option b) tempts students because Kandel showed that gene expression is involved, but switching genes on inside a neuron during learning does not pass the change to offspring.", "topic": "Kandel and synaptic plasticity", "fig": "Eric Kandel"}, {"q": "Which of the following is due to the regeneration of rhodopsin?", "options": {"a": "Lateral inhibition", "b": "Light Adaptation", "c": "Stereopsis", "d": "Dark Adaptation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: dark adaptation is due to the regeneration of rhodopsin. Rhodopsin is the light-sensitive pigment of the rods, the receptors for dim-light (scotopic) vision. It is made of a protein, opsin, joined to retinal, a form of vitamin A. When a photon strikes it, retinal changes shape and the molecule breaks apart (bleaches), which starts the neural signal; a bleached molecule cannot respond again until it is rebuilt, and rebuilding is slow. In bright daylight most rhodopsin is bleached, so the rods contribute almost nothing. Walk into a dark cinema and at first you see nothing. Over the next minutes sensitivity rises in two stages: the cones adapt quickly and level off after about 5 to 10 minutes; then comes a kink in the curve, the rod-cone break, and the rods, as their rhodopsin regenerates, take over and keep improving until full sensitivity at about 20 to 30 minutes, when the eye can be many thousands of times more sensitive than at the start. Vitamin A deficiency slows rhodopsin regeneration and causes night blindness. When Tara enters a dark auditorium she stumbles at first and later reads the seat numbers: that is her rhodopsin coming back. Option d is right because the slow return of sensitivity in the dark is caused by the rebuilding of the rod pigment. Exam trap: light adaptation is the paired process, so students choose it; but it is the fast fall in sensitivity on entering bright light, caused by bleaching of pigment, the opposite of regeneration.", "topic": "Rhodopsin and dark adaptation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Regions and boundaries are central to personality theory of:", "options": {"a": "George Kelly", "b": "Karen Horney", "c": "Kurt Lewin", "d": "McClelland"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: regions and boundaries are central to the personality theory of Kurt Lewin (1936). In Principles of Topological Psychology, Lewin built a field theory of personality and behaviour out of Gestalt ideas. His formula B = f(P, E) says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment, which together make up the life space: all the facts that influence a person at one moment, as that person sees them. Lewin borrowed topology, a branch of geometry that deals with spatial relations, to draw the life space as a map of regions. Each region is an area of possible activity (studying, friends, family) or a part of the person, who is divided into inner-personal regions (needs) and a perceptual-motor region that deals with the world. Regions are separated by boundaries that can be firm or permeable: across a firm boundary, tension in one region does not spill into the next. Moving from one region to another is called locomotion; regions carry positive or negative valences, forces are drawn as vectors, and barriers block paths. As a child grows, the life space becomes more differentiated, with more regions. Divya, who wants to join a dance class (a positive valence) but must first get past her parents' refusal (a barrier), is a classic Lewinian picture. Option c is right because the language of regions, boundaries, locomotion, valences and vectors belongs to Lewin's topological psychology. Exam trap: George Kelly's (1955) personal construct theory also uses spatial-sounding terms such as 'range of convenience', but its units are bipolar constructs, not regions and boundaries.", "topic": "Lewin's topological psychology", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "Autistic disorder is linked to a deficiency in:", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Cingulate gyrus", "c": "Hippocampus", "d": "Mirror Neuron System"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: autistic disorder has been linked to a deficiency in the mirror neuron system. Mirror neurons were found by Giacomo Rizzolatti and colleagues in the 1990s in area F5 of the macaque's ventral premotor cortex: the same cells fired when the monkey grasped a piece of food and when it watched a person grasp one. In humans a mirror system is described in the inferior frontal gyrus (including Broca's area), premotor cortex and inferior parietal lobule. Because it maps another person's action on to one's own motor plan, it was proposed as a basis for imitation, understanding others' intentions and empathy. Autism involves difficulties in exactly these areas: poor imitation, weak joint attention and trouble reading other minds. This led to the 'broken mirror' hypothesis. Lindsay Oberman, Vilayanur Ramachandran and colleagues (2005) reported that the EEG mu rhythm, which is suppressed when typical people watch actions, was not suppressed in autistic participants, and Mirella Dapretto and colleagues (2006) found less inferior frontal mirror activity when autistic children imitated emotional faces, with the least activity in the most impaired children. Six-year-old Ishaan, who does not copy his teacher's clapping game or smile back at her, shows the kind of imitation gap the theory set out to explain. Option d is right because, of these options, the mirror neuron system is the one specifically proposed as deficient to explain autism's imitation and social difficulties. Exam trap: mirror neurons fire for both doing and watching an action, which is why a motor system came to be linked to a social disorder; the amygdala, a genuine rival account, is the most tempting wrong choice. Key note: Simon Baron-Cohen's amygdala theory (2000) also links autism to the amygdala, and the broken mirror account is now disputed, since many later studies find intact imitation and mirror responses in autism; the question uses the mirror neuron account; d stands.", "topic": "Broken mirror theory of autism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The onset of dreams is due to the increased activity of:", "options": {"a": "Locus Coeruleus", "b": "Raphe nuclei", "c": "Caudate nuclei", "d": "Hippocampus"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the classic account, the onset of dreaming (REM) sleep is due to increased activity of the locus coeruleus. Dreams occur mostly in REM sleep, discovered by Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953): rapid eye movements, a waking-like EEG and loss of muscle tone, with vivid, story-like dreams reported on waking. Michel Jouvet, working on cats in Lyon from the late 1950s, named it paradoxical sleep and showed (1962) that it is generated in the pons: cats with the forebrain removed still cycled into it. In his monoamine theory of sleep, serotonin from the raphe nuclei starts slow-wave sleep, and the locus coeruleus, a small blue-pigmented nucleus in the dorsal pons that is the brain's main source of noradrenaline, triggers paradoxical sleep, the phase of vivid dreams. Jouvet's lesions in the locus coeruleus region abolished the muscle atonia of REM, so the cats got up during that sleep and seemed to act out their dreams, stalking and attacking unseen prey. When Nikhil's eyes start darting under his lids about 90 minutes after he falls asleep, he has entered the first REM period of the night. Option a is right because, in Jouvet's textbook account, rising locus coeruleus activity switches the brain from slow-wave sleep into REM sleep, when dreams begin. Exam trap: the raphe nuclei are the other half of the same theory, but they start slow-wave sleep, not dreaming. Key note: modern work (J. Allan Hobson and Robert McCarley, 1975 reciprocal interaction model) shows locus coeruleus noradrenaline and raphe serotonin neurons fall silent in REM ('REM-off'), while cholinergic pontine neurons (PPT, LDT) and the sublaterodorsal (subcoeruleus) area beside the locus coeruleus are 'REM-on'; the question follows Jouvet's textbook account; a stands.", "topic": "Brainstem control of REM dreaming", "fig": "Michel Jouvet"}, {"q": "A non-invasive procedure capable of revealing the neural activity of the regions of the brain:", "options": {"a": "Positron Emission Tomography", "b": "Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "c": "Computerised Tomography", "d": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) is the non-invasive procedure that reveals the neural activity of brain regions. MRI places the head in a strong magnetic field and uses radio waves to read signals from hydrogen nuclei, so no X-rays or other ionising radiation are involved. fMRI adds the time dimension. When a brain region becomes active, local blood flow rises more than the neurons' extra oxygen use, so the region ends up with relatively more oxygenated haemoglobin. Oxygenated and deoxygenated haemoglobin differ in their magnetic properties, and this difference gives the blood oxygenation level dependent (BOLD) signal described by Seiji Ogawa and colleagues (1990). Scanning every second or two while a person does a task shows which regions become more active. Nothing is injected and nothing is radioactive, so the method is non-invasive and can be repeated many times. Its spatial resolution is good (a few millimetres) but its timing is slow (seconds), because blood flow lags behind neural firing. When Aisha looks at photographs of faces in the scanner, the BOLD signal rises in her fusiform face area. Option b is right because fMRI is the only option that both shows activity, not just structure, and needs no injection or radiation. Exam trap: PET also shows brain activity, so students pick option a; but PET needs a radioactive tracer such as fluorodeoxyglucose injected into the blood, so it is invasive. The two structural scans, CT and plain MRI, are the other trap: MRI is non-invasive but shows only anatomy.", "topic": "Functional brain imaging", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An antagonist to AcetylCholine (Ach):", "options": {"a": "Tropisetron", "b": "Curare", "c": "Metrazol", "d": "Clonidine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: curare is an antagonist to acetylcholine (ACh). An antagonist is a drug that blocks or reduces the action of a neurotransmitter, often by sitting on its receptor without activating it. ACh is the transmitter at the neuromuscular junction, where motor neurons make skeletal muscle contract, and there it acts on nicotinic receptors, which are ion channels. Curare, an arrow poison of South American hunters, is a plant extract whose main active part, d-tubocurarine, binds to these nicotinic receptors and keeps ACh off them, so the muscles cannot respond. The result is limp paralysis; death comes from paralysis of the breathing muscles while the mind stays clear. Hunters could still eat the prey safely because curare is poorly absorbed from the gut. Claude Bernard (1850s) showed with frog nerve-muscle preparations that curare acts at the junction between nerve and muscle, not on the nerve or the muscle itself. Curare-like drugs later became muscle relaxants in surgery and in modified electroconvulsive therapy. Other ACh antagonists act elsewhere: atropine and scopolamine block muscarinic receptors, and botulinum toxin works differently again, by stopping ACh release. Option b is right because curare is the classic textbook example of a drug that blocks acetylcholine at its nicotinic receptors. Exam trap: clonidine lowers activity in a transmitter system, so it sounds like an antagonist, but it is an agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors that reduces noradrenaline release; and Metrazol acts on the GABA system, not on ACh.", "topic": "Acetylcholine antagonists", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which statements are true about Reliability and Validity: 1. Reliability and validity are two dimensions of test efficiency 2. The requirements of high reliability and high validity are opposite to each other 3. The validity of the test is always higher than the index of reliability 4. Validity may be high even without high reliability. Choose the correct option:", "options": {"a": "1, 2 and 4 are correct", "b": "1, 2 and 3 are correct", "c": "1, 2 and 2 are correct", "d": "1, 3 and 4 are correct"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 2 are correct (option c is printed '1, 2 and 2', meaning 1 and 2 only). Statement 1 is true: reliability (consistency of scores) and validity (accuracy for the intended purpose) are the two basic dimensions of a test's efficiency, the twin standards every test manual must report. Statement 2 is true in a specific technical sense, the attenuation paradox discussed by Harold Gulliksen (1950) and Jane Loevinger (1954). To push internal consistency up, a test maker keeps items that correlate highly with one another, so the test becomes narrow and homogeneous. To push validity against a broad, complex criterion (job success, school achievement) up, the best items are those that each correlate with the criterion but only weakly with one another, so each adds something new. Beyond a point, then, the two demands pull in opposite directions. Statement 3 is false: validity can never exceed the index of reliability, the square root of the reliability coefficient, because a test cannot correlate with anything else more than it correlates with its own true score. If Kavya's new aptitude test has a reliability of 0.81, its validity cannot exceed 0.90. Statement 4 is false: a test full of random error cannot correlate well with anything, so it cannot be highly valid without being reliable; reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps 1 and 2 and drops both false statements, 3 and 4. Exam trap: statement 3 reverses the true rule; the index of reliability is the ceiling for validity, not its floor. Key note: option c is printed '1, 2 and 2 are correct', plainly a misprint for '1 and 2 are correct'; c stands.", "topic": "Reliability and validity relations", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List I (Neurological condition) with List II (Brain site): A. Finger agnosia, B. Dressing apraxia, C. General amnesic syndrome, D. Intermanual conflict. 1. Bilateral medial temporal lesions, 2. Right posterior parieto-temporal area, 3. Left posterior brain, 4. Cerebral commissurotomy", "options": {"a": "A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4", "b": "A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4", "c": "A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1", "d": "A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4. Finger agnosia (A) is the inability to name, point to or tell apart one's own or another person's fingers. It follows damage to the left posterior brain, at the angular gyrus of the parietal lobe, Josef Gerstmann (1924) first described it, and it became the core of his syndrome with agraphia, acalculia and left-right confusion: so A-3. Dressing apraxia (B) is difficulty putting on clothes, with sleeves, buttons and the left and right sides muddled, from a breakdown of spatial and body-schema processing. It goes with right posterior parieto-temporal damage, often alongside left-sided neglect and constructional apraxia: so B-2. The general amnesic syndrome (C), a dense anterograde amnesia with intact intelligence and short-term memory, follows bilateral medial temporal lesions of the hippocampus and nearby cortex, as in the patient H.M. after William Scoville's (1953) operation, studied by Brenda Milner (1957): so C-1. Intermanual conflict (D), in which one hand undoes what the other does (one buttons a shirt while the other unbuttons it), is a callosal sign after cerebral commissurotomy, the split-brain operation studied by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s), and is a form of alien hand behaviour: so D-4. When Ramesh, after a right parietal stroke, puts his shirt on inside out and cannot find the left sleeve, that is dressing apraxia. Option b is right because it gives exactly these four pairs. Exam trap: the two parietal syndromes are easily swapped, so remember that finger agnosia is left (with the language-linked Gerstmann signs) and dressing apraxia is right (with spatial neglect); option a makes exactly that swap.", "topic": "Localising neuropsychological syndromes", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"imhans-2020": [{"q": "The occurrence of \"Illusory conjunctions\" is evidence confirming the:", "concept": "The answer is c: illusory conjunctions are the classic evidence for the feature integration theory of Anne Treisman and Garry Gelade (1980). The theory says that seeing an object happens in two stages. In the first, pre-attentive stage, simple features such as colour, orientation, curvature and size are registered automatically and in parallel across the whole visual field, each in its own feature map. In the second stage, focused attention moves to one location at a time and binds the features found there into a single object; attention is the glue. If attention is overloaded or diverted, the features are still registered but float free, so they can be combined wrongly. Anne Treisman and Hilary Schmidt (1982) briefly flashed three coloured letters (for example a green N, a red X and a blue O) flanked by two black digits, which had to be reported first; people often reported a red N or a blue X, an object that was never shown. These errors are illusory conjunctions. The same theory explains visual search: a target defined by one feature pops out, while a target defined by a conjunction of features needs slow, serial search. Example: Riya glances at a busy street while chatting and later remembers a yellow scooter, when the scooter was black and the yellow belonged to a taxi beside it. Option c is right because only feature integration theory says features are coded separately and need attention to be bound, which is exactly what an illusory conjunction shows when binding fails. Exam trap: Pandemonium also breaks patterns into features, so students pick it, but it has no attention-binding stage and does not predict miscombined features.", "traps": {"a": "Pandemonium is Oliver Selfridge's (1959) model of pattern recognition, in which layers of 'demons' detect features and shout for the letter they match. It is a feature analysis model with no stage where attention binds features, so it does not predict illusory conjunctions.", "b": "Prototype theory, from Eleanor Rosch (1973, 1975), says we classify objects by comparing them with the most typical example of a category. It explains typicality effects in categorisation, not errors in combining colour and shape.", "d": "Bottleneck (filter) models, starting with Donald Broadbent (1958), say unattended information is blocked at a single early filter on the basis of physical features. They explain why unattended messages are lost, not why features are wrongly bound together."}}, {"q": "The ERP (Event-Related Potential) component that occurs when the form of a sentence is incorrect:", "concept": "The answer is a: the P600 is the event-related potential (ERP) component evoked when the grammatical form of a sentence is wrong. An ERP is the small voltage change in the EEG that is time-locked to a stimulus. Because a single trial is buried in background EEG, hundreds of trials are averaged, and the waves that remain are named by polarity (P for positive, N for negative) and by latency in milliseconds. In language research two components matter most. The N400 is a negative wave peaking about 400 ms after a word whose meaning does not fit the sentence, first reported by Marta Kutas and Steven Hillyard (1980) with sentences such as 'He spread the warm bread with socks'. The P600, also called the syntactic positive shift, is a positive wave beginning about 500 ms and peaking near 600 ms after a word that breaks the rules of grammar, such as a wrong verb agreement, or a garden-path sentence that has to be re-parsed. Lee Osterhout and Phillip Holcomb (1992) and Peter Hagoort, Colin Brown and Jolanda Groothusen (1993) described it. Example: if Kabir reads 'The boys eats mangoes', a P600 appears at 'eats'; if he reads 'The boys ate their shoes for lunch', an N400 appears at 'shoes'. Option a is right because the form of a sentence means its syntax, and syntactic violations produce the P600. Exam trap: the N400 is the better-known language component, so students choose it; remember N400 for meaning (semantics) and P600 for grammar (syntax).", "traps": {"b": "The N400 (Marta Kutas and Steven Hillyard, 1980) is a negative wave about 400 ms after a word whose meaning does not fit the sentence. It marks semantic anomaly, not an error in the grammatical form of the sentence.", "c": "P400 is not a standard language ERP component. The well-known positive waves are the P300 (attention to a rare target in an oddball task) and the P600 (syntax), so P400 is a made-up near miss.", "d": "N600 is not a recognised language component either. It blends the polarity of the N400 with the latency of the P600, a mix meant to catch students who half remember both."}}, {"q": "Research on the phenomenon of memory consolidation can be traced to the pioneering work of:", "concept": "The answer is b: research on memory consolidation goes back to Georg Elias Müller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900), who coined the term. Consolidation is the process by which a new, fragile memory trace becomes stable and long lasting over time. Müller and Pilzecker had people learn lists of nonsense syllable pairs and found that recall was poorer when a second list was learned soon after the first than when a rest interval came in between. They argued that the neural activity set up by learning carries on for a while after practice ends (perseveration) and that this continued activity fixes the trace; new learning during that window disrupts it. This was the perseveration-consolidation hypothesis, and it was also the first clear demonstration of retroactive interference. Later evidence fits the idea: a head injury or electroconvulsive therapy wipes out memories of the minutes before the event, while older memories survive, and Théodule Ribot (1881) had already noted that recent memories are lost before old ones. Today consolidation is studied at the synapse (protein synthesis) and at the systems level (hippocampus to cortex, helped by sleep). Example: Meera revises her polity notes and then immediately crams economics; next day her polity recall is weaker than if she had rested, because the second session disturbed consolidation. Option b is right because Müller and Pilzecker both introduced the term consolidation and ran the first experiments on it. Exam trap: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) is the famous memory pioneer, so students pick him, but he measured forgetting and savings, not consolidation.", "traps": {"a": "Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) founded the experimental study of memory with nonsense syllables, the forgetting curve and the savings method. He showed how fast material is forgotten but did not propose consolidation.", "c": "Franciscus Donders (1868) devised the subtractive method of reaction time, subtracting simple from choice reaction time to time a mental stage. That is mental chronometry, not memory consolidation.", "d": "William James (1890) distinguished primary memory (what is still in consciousness) from secondary memory (the long-term store) in The Principles of Psychology. That foreshadows the short-term and long-term distinction, not research on consolidation."}}, {"q": "In a simple linear regression equation, b₀ represents the:", "concept": "The answer is b: in a simple linear regression equation, b0 is the estimated intercept. Simple linear regression fits a straight line that predicts one variable (Y, the response or criterion) from one other variable (X, the explanatory or predictor variable). The fitted equation is written Y-hat = b0 + b1X. Y-hat is the estimated or predicted response. b1 is the estimated slope: how much Y-hat changes for every one-unit rise in X. b0 is the estimated intercept, the constant: the predicted value of Y when X equals 0, the point where the line crosses the Y axis. Both are found by the method of least squares, which chooses the line that makes the sum of the squared vertical distances (residuals) between the points and the line as small as possible. The slope is b1 = r(SDy / SDx), and the intercept is then b0 = mean of Y minus b1 times mean of X, so the line always passes through the point given by the two means. Greek letters (beta0 and beta1) stand for the population values; b0 and b1 are the sample estimates, which is why the options say 'estimated'. Example: Arjun's teacher finds that exam score = 35 + 5 x hours of study. Here 35 is b0, the score predicted for a student who studies for 0 hours, and 5 is b1, five extra marks for each extra hour. Option b is right because b0 is the constant term that fixes where the line starts on the Y axis. Exam trap: distractors swap slope and intercept; the slope is the coefficient that multiplies X, while the intercept stands alone.", "traps": {"a": "The estimated or predicted response is Y-hat, the value on the left of the equation that the line gives for a particular X. b0 is only one part of that prediction (its value when X = 0).", "c": "The estimated slope is b1, the coefficient that multiplies X and tells how much Y-hat changes per unit of X. b0 does not multiply anything.", "d": "'Explanatory' describes X, the explanatory or predictor variable whose values are used to predict Y. b0 is a fitted constant, not a variable."}}, {"q": "The F+ response in the Rorschach protocol exceeding 80% is indicative of:", "concept": "The answer is a: an F+ percentage above 80 in a Rorschach record is read as greater ego strength, that is, sound reality testing and ego control. In Rorschach scoring each response is coded for its determinant, the feature of the blot that produced it. F means the response was based on form (shape) alone, with no colour, shading or movement. Each pure form response is then judged for form quality: F+ if the percept fits the shape of the blot well, F minus if the fit is poor or arbitrary. Hermann Rorschach (1921) introduced this scoring, and F+% (good pure form responses divided by all pure form responses, times 100) became, in the systems of Bruno Klopfer (1942) and Samuel Beck (1944), the main index of how accurately a person sees reality. A high F+% (about 80 and above) means the person checks inner ideas against the outer world and keeps impulses under control, the classical meaning of an intact, strong ego. A low F+% (below about 60 to 70) means percepts are driven by inner preoccupations, as in psychosis or organic brain disorder. Example: Kabir, a stable student, sees a bat and two dancers where the shapes really fit, giving an F+% near 85. Option a is right because a high F+% shows accurate, controlled perception, which is ego strength. Exam trap: severe psychopathology is the opposite pole; it goes with a low F+%, not a high one. Key note: a very high F+% together with a high proportion of pure form responses (F%) and few colour or movement responses is read as rigid over-control and constriction, so very high values are not simply better; but of these options, greater ego strength is the textbook reading, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Severe psychopathology, especially psychosis or organic disorder, shows as a low F+%, when many responses ignore the real shape of the blot. A high F+% points the other way.", "c": "Impulsiveness on the Rorschach shows as pure colour (C) and colour-form (CF) responses with poor form, where emotion overrides control. A high proportion of good form responses shows control, not impulsivity.", "d": "Internalisation is not a Rorschach form-quality interpretation. The nearest Rorschach idea, an introversive style (more human movement than colour), comes from the Experience Balance, not from F+%."}}, {"q": "A Statistical procedure for determining the relationship between two or more independent variables and two or more dependent variables is:", "concept": "The answer is c: canonical analysis (canonical correlation analysis) is the procedure for relating two or more independent variables to two or more dependent variables at the same time. It was developed by Harold Hotelling (1936). Ordinary correlation links one X with one Y, and multiple regression links several Xs with one Y. Canonical analysis goes a step further: it takes a whole set of predictors and a whole set of criteria, forms a weighted sum (a linear combination, called a canonical variate) of each set, and chooses the weights so that the correlation between the two sums is as large as possible. That correlation is the first canonical correlation. The procedure then finds a second pair of variates, uncorrelated with the first pair, and so on, up to the number of variables in the smaller set. Each canonical correlation is tested for significance (often with Wilks' lambda), and the loadings show which variables carry each link. Multiple regression, ANOVA, MANOVA and discriminant analysis can all be treated as special cases of it. Example: Ananya measures three family variables (warmth, conflict, parental education) and four adolescent outcomes (self-esteem, grades, anxiety, peer relations). Canonical analysis tells her how the family set as a whole relates to the outcome set as a whole, instead of running twelve separate correlations. Option c is right because it is the only option built for many predictors and many outcomes together. Exam trap: multiple regression also handles several variables, but it allows only one dependent variable; with two or more DVs you need canonical analysis (or MANOVA when the IVs are groups).", "traps": {"a": "Cluster analysis groups cases (or variables) into clusters of similar members using a distance measure. It has no split into independent and dependent variables, so it cannot test how one set relates to another.", "b": "Multiple regression relates several independent variables to a single dependent variable. Once there are two or more dependent variables, it no longer fits the stem.", "d": "Survival analysis models the time until an event happens (relapse, death, dropout), for example with Kaplan-Meier curves or the proportional hazards regression of David Cox (1972). It has one outcome, time to the event, not a set of DVs."}}, {"q": "The taming effect in Kluver-Bucy syndrome is attributed to lesions in the following:", "concept": "The answer is d: the taming effect in Klüver-Bucy syndrome is attributed to loss of the amygdala. Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both anterior temporal lobes of rhesus monkeys. Previously wild, fearful animals became tame and placid: they approached snakes and humans without fear. The monkeys also showed psychic blindness or visual agnosia (they saw objects but did not recognise their meaning), hyperorality (putting everything in the mouth), changes in diet, hypersexuality and hypermetamorphosis (an urge to attend to and touch everything in sight). Because the lesions were large, the next question was which structure caused which sign. Lawrence Weiskrantz (1956) made smaller lesions limited to the amygdala and found that amygdala removal alone produced the taming. This fits the amygdala's role in detecting threat, in fear conditioning and in giving objects their emotional value. In humans the syndrome appears after herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia. Example: if Arjun's laboratory monkey were given bilateral amygdala lesions, it would stop shrieking at a rubber snake and might even pick it up. Option d is right because the amygdala is the structure whose removal abolishes fear and aggression, the core of the taming effect. Exam trap: the hippocampus lies beside the amygdala in the same temporal lobe and was also removed, so students choose it; its loss affects memory, not fear. Key note: the paper prints 'training effect', which is plainly a misprint of 'taming effect' (placidity and loss of fear), the reading shown in the stem here; on that reading, d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The hippocampus, removed along with the amygdala in the original lobectomies, is needed for forming new declarative memories, as patient H.M. showed. Its damage gives amnesia, not tameness.", "b": "The presubiculum is part of the hippocampal formation, a relay between the subiculum and the entorhinal cortex that contains head-direction cells. It serves spatial orientation and memory, not fear.", "c": "The fornix is the fibre bundle that carries output from the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies in the Papez circuit. Its damage causes memory problems, not loss of fear."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements regarding Collins and Quillian's model are true? 1. It is a hierarchical model 2. Back propagation is a feature of this model 3. The model explains the typicality effect 4. The lexical decision task validates the model", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 4 are true. Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) proposed the hierarchical network model of semantic memory. Concepts are nodes arranged in levels (ANIMAL above BIRD above CANARY) joined by 'is a' links, and each property is stored once, at the highest node it applies to (cognitive economy): 'has skin' at ANIMAL, 'can fly' at BIRD, 'is yellow' at CANARY. They tested it with sentence verification: people took longer to verify 'A canary has skin' than 'A canary can fly', as if moving up more levels. So statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: back propagation is the learning rule of connectionist (parallel distributed processing) networks, described by David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams (1986). Statement 3 is false: the model could not explain the typicality effect, that 'A robin is a bird' is verified faster than 'A penguin is a bird' though both are one level apart (Lance Rips, Edward Shoben and Edward Smith, 1973). That failure led to the feature comparison model (Edward Smith, Edward Shoben and Lance Rips, 1974) and the spreading activation model (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975). Statement 4: semantic priming in the lexical decision task (David Meyer and Roger Schvaneveldt, 1971), where 'nurse' is recognised faster after 'doctor', is the classic evidence for network models. Example: Meera says yes to 'A sparrow is a bird' faster than to 'An ostrich is a bird', the typicality effect the model cannot explain. Option c is right because only 1 and 4 survive. Exam trap: students credit the model with typicality, its best-known failure. Key note: strictly, Collins and Quillian used sentence verification, and lexical decision priming supports semantic networks in general (the 1975 revision most of all), so statement 4 is the loosest; but 3 is plainly false and 2 belongs to connectionism, so 1 and 4 is the only defensible pair, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "2 and 3 combines the two false statements: back propagation belongs to connectionist models (David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams, 1986), and the typicality effect is the very finding the hierarchical model could not explain.", "b": "2 and 4 keeps the lexical decision evidence but wrongly adds back propagation, the learning rule of PDP networks, and drops statement 1, the defining hierarchical structure of the model.", "d": "1 and 3 is right about the hierarchy but wrongly claims the model explains typicality; robin and penguin sit at the same level, so the model predicts equal times, yet the typical robin is verified faster."}}, {"q": "Akinetic mutism results from lesions in which part of the frontal lobe:", "concept": "The answer is b: akinetic mutism results from lesions of the medial frontal lobe. Akinetic mutism is a state in which the patient is awake and follows the examiner with the eyes, yet shows almost no spontaneous movement or speech. Sensory and motor pathways are intact, so with strong urging the patient may move or say a word; what is lost is the drive to act, an extreme form of abulia (lack of will and initiative). Hugh Cairns and colleagues (1941) named the state in a patient with a cyst in the third ventricle. The typical lesion is bilateral damage to the medial frontal cortex, especially the anterior cingulate cortex and the supplementary motor area, often from infarcts of both anterior cerebral arteries (which supply the medial surface of the hemispheres) or a midline tumour. The anterior cingulate links motivation with action, which is why its loss leaves a frozen but conscious person. Learn the three prefrontal syndromes as a set: dorsolateral damage gives the dysexecutive syndrome, orbitofrontal damage gives disinhibition, and medial damage gives apathy, abulia and, at its worst, akinetic mutism. Example: after strokes in both anterior cerebral arteries, Rohan lies with his eyes open and tracks his mother around the room, but does not speak or move unless prompted again and again. Option b is right because the medial frontal and anterior cingulate regions supply the drive to start movement and speech. Exam trap: 'prefrontal' sounds right because the syndrome is frontal, but it is the umbrella term for all three regions, not the specific site.", "traps": {"a": "Dorsolateral prefrontal damage produces the dysexecutive syndrome: poor planning, set shifting and abstraction, with perseveration on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. These patients still move and speak spontaneously.", "c": "Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition, impulsivity, tactlessness and poor social judgement, as in Phineas Gage. The patient is socially over-active, the opposite of akinetic mutism.", "d": "Prefrontal is the umbrella term covering the dorsolateral, orbitofrontal and medial regions together. It is too broad to name the specific site of akinetic mutism."}}, {"q": "The testing effects occurring for a within-subject variable are overcome through:", "concept": "The answer is a: testing effects are overcome by the Solomon four-group design, introduced by Richard Solomon (1949). A testing effect occurs when taking a pretest itself changes scores on the posttest, through practice, familiarity or sensitisation to the topic. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) list testing as a threat to internal validity, and they add a related threat to external validity: the pretest can interact with the treatment, making people more responsive to it, so results may not apply to people who were never pretested. The pretest is a within-subject element, because the same person is measured twice. The Solomon design crosses pretest (yes or no) with treatment (yes or no): group 1 gets pretest, treatment and posttest; group 2 gets pretest and posttest only; group 3 gets treatment and posttest; group 4 gets the posttest alone. Comparing pretested with unpretested groups estimates the testing effect, and comparing the treatment effect across them reveals the pretest-by-treatment interaction, so each can be measured and separated. Its cost is that it needs twice as many participants. Example: Riya tests whether an anti-stigma talk changes students' attitudes to mental illness. The pretest questionnaire might itself make students think about stigma; the two unpretested groups show how much of the change came from the questionnaire. Option a is right because only this design measures testing effects directly instead of hoping they cancel out. Exam trap: an ordinary control group also takes the pretest, so it equalises the main testing effect but cannot detect the pretest-by-treatment interaction.", "traps": {"b": "A double-blind design keeps both participants and experimenters unaware of who receives the treatment, controlling expectancy effects such as placebo responses and experimenter bias. It does nothing about the influence of a pretest.", "c": "A control group that also takes the pretest equalises the main testing effect across groups but cannot measure it or detect a pretest-by-treatment interaction; the Solomon design adds the unpretested groups to do that.", "d": "Unobtrusive observation (Eugene Webb and colleagues, 1966) controls reactivity, people changing because they know they are being watched. It is a measurement strategy, not a design that separates out pretest effects."}}, {"q": "Which of the following can be used to identify interaction effects when the predictor and moderator variables are nominal, and the dependent variable is a score variable?", "concept": "The answer is a: when the predictor and the moderator are both nominal (categorical) and the dependent variable is a score, the interaction is tested with ANOVA. A moderator is a variable that changes the direction or strength of the relation between a predictor and an outcome; it tells us when, or for whom, an effect holds. Reuben Baron and David Kenny (1986) set out how to test moderation, and the method depends on the level of measurement of the variables. If both predictor and moderator are categorical and the outcome is continuous, moderation is simply the interaction term in a factorial (for example 2 by 2) analysis of variance. The main effect of each factor is tested, and a significant interaction F shows that the effect of one factor differs across the levels of the other, which is what moderation means. If the predictor or the moderator is continuous, moderated multiple regression is used instead: the two variables are entered, then their product term, and a significant gain in variance explained shows moderation. Example: Kabir compares therapy with a waiting list (the predictor) in men and women (the moderator), with depression scores as the outcome. A two-way ANOVA tells him whether therapy works, whether the sexes differ, and, through the interaction, whether therapy helps one sex more than the other. Option a is right because ANOVA is the standard test of an interaction between nominal factors on a score DV. Exam trap: chi-square handles nominal variables, so it tempts, but it needs a nominal outcome as well, and here the outcome is a score.", "traps": {"b": "Discriminant analysis (Ronald Fisher, 1936) predicts membership of a categorical group from continuous predictors. Its DV is nominal, the reverse of what this stem describes.", "c": "Chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900) tests association between categorical variables using frequency counts. There is no score DV in a chi-square test, so it cannot test an interaction on scores.", "d": "Logistic regression predicts a categorical (usually binary) outcome, such as relapse or no relapse, from predictors of any type. It suits a nominal DV, not a score DV."}}, {"q": "The motor pathway that controls hand and finger movements:", "concept": "The answer is b: the corticospinal pathway, specifically the lateral corticospinal tract, controls hand and finger movements. This tract begins in the primary motor cortex of the precentral gyrus, including the giant Betz cells of layer V, and descends through the internal capsule and the cerebral peduncles to the pyramids of the medulla, which is why it is also called the pyramidal tract. At the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla about 85 to 90 per cent of its fibres cross, so each hemisphere moves the opposite side of the body. The crossed fibres run in the lateral column of the spinal cord, and in primates many of them synapse directly on the motor neurons that supply the muscles of the fingers. This direct link allows fractionated, independent finger movements, such as writing or picking up a needle. Monkeys whose pyramidal tracts have been cut can still walk, climb and grasp with the whole hand, but they lose the ability to move the fingers one at a time. The hand also has a large share of the motor homunculus. Example: when Meera threads a needle or types a text message, her lateral corticospinal tract is doing the fine work. Option b is right because the lateral corticospinal tract is the only descending pathway with direct connections to the finger motor neurons. Exam trap: the rubrospinal tract also controls limb movements, including the hand, so students choose it; but it moves the forearm and hand as a unit and cannot give independent finger control.", "traps": {"a": "The corticobulbar pathway runs from the motor cortex to the motor nuclei of the cranial nerves in the brainstem. It controls the muscles of the face, jaw, tongue and throat, not the fingers.", "c": "The ventromedial pathways (ventral corticospinal, vestibulospinal, tectospinal and reticulospinal tracts) act on the trunk and proximal limb muscles for posture, balance and walking. They do not control fine distal movements.", "d": "The rubrospinal tract, from the red nucleus of the midbrain, belongs to the dorsolateral system and helps move the forearms and hands. It is a secondary route that cannot produce independent finger movements."}}, {"q": "The two-point code is a unique feature of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the two-point code is a feature of the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI), published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). The MMPI has 10 clinical scales, numbered 1 to 0 (1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania, 0 Social Introversion), plus validity scales. Raw scores are turned into T scores, and clinicians read the whole profile rather than single scales. In code-type interpretation, the numbers of the two highest elevated clinical scales are written as a two-point code: for example 2-7 (anxious, worried depression), 4-9 (impulsive, antisocial acting out) or 1-3 (physical complaints under stress). Research has gathered the typical traits of people with each code, so a code works as shorthand for a well-studied personality pattern; three-point codes extend the idea. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 shows Depression at T 78 and Psychasthenia at T 72 as his two highest scales, so his profile is coded 2-7, suggesting a tense, self-critical, depressed man. Option a is right because only the MMPI reports results as codes built from the numbers of the highest clinical scales. Exam trap: the Luria-Nebraska also has numbered clinical scales, so students choose it, but its standard reading compares each scale with a critical level for brain damage; the two-point code is the hallmark of the MMPI.", "traps": {"b": "The Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961) is a 21-item self-report scale that gives a single total score for the severity of depression. It has no profile and no code types.", "c": "The Halstead-Reitan Battery (Ward Halstead, 1947, and Ralph Reitan) is a neuropsychological battery summarised by the Halstead Impairment Index, the proportion of tests in the impaired range. It does not use two-point codes.", "d": "The Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery (Charles Golden, 1978) has 11 clinical scales (motor, rhythm, tactile, visual and so on) compared with a critical level for brain damage. Its standard reading asks which and how many scales exceed that level; two-point code types are the hallmark of the MMPI."}}, {"q": "The Go/No-Go test is a bed side test for assessing the function of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Go/No-Go test is a bedside test of frontal lobe function. The patient must respond to one signal and hold back the response to another, for example: 'tap once when I tap once; do not tap when I tap twice'. The go trials are easy; the hard part is the no-go trial, which needs response inhibition, the ability to stop a prepared or habitual action. Response inhibition is an executive function that depends on the prefrontal cortex, especially the right inferior frontal gyrus and the pre-supplementary motor area, and on their links with the basal ganglia. Patients with frontal damage keep tapping on no-go trials or simply copy the examiner (echopraxia), showing a loss of inhibitory control. Alexander Luria (1966) used such conflicting and go/no-go instructions in his clinical examination of frontal patients, and the Frontal Assessment Battery of Bruno Dubois and colleagues (2000) includes a go/no-go item along with similarities, verbal fluency, the fist-edge-palm motor sequence, conflicting instructions and a test of grasping behaviour. Example: Kabir, who has had a frontal head injury, is asked to raise a finger at one tap and stay still at two; he raises it every time. Option b is right because the test probes the inhibition of a prepotent response, a classic frontal executive function. Exam trap: the task is sometimes mistaken for a test of attention or memory, and so of the temporal or parietal lobes, but its core deficit is failed inhibition, which is frontal.", "traps": {"a": "The temporal lobes handle memory (the medial temporal lobe and hippocampus), hearing and language comprehension (Wernicke's area). They are tested with word list learning, recall and naming, not with go/no-go tasks.", "c": "The parietal lobes handle spatial attention, the body schema and praxis. Bedside tests for them are line bisection and cancellation for neglect and gesture imitation for apraxia.", "d": "The occipital lobe is visual cortex, tested through visual fields, colour and object recognition. Failing to withhold a response on a go/no-go trial is not a visual deficit."}}, {"q": "The assumption that pairs of scores from the same individuals in the population have equal variance is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the assumption that pairs of scores from the same individuals have equal variance (of their differences) is the sphericity assumption. Sphericity belongs to repeated-measures (within-subjects) ANOVA, in which the same people are measured under three or more conditions. For every pair of conditions you can compute each person's difference score (condition A minus B, A minus C, B minus C). Sphericity requires that the population variances of all these difference scores are equal. When it holds, the F ratio follows the F distribution correctly; when it is violated, the F test becomes too liberal and Type I errors rise. It is tested with Mauchly's test (John Mauchly, 1940); a significant result means the assumption is violated, and the degrees of freedom are then reduced by the Greenhouse-Geisser correction (more conservative) or the Huynh-Feldt correction (less so), or a multivariate test such as MANOVA is used instead. Sphericity cannot be violated with only two conditions, because there is only one set of difference scores. Example: Meera measures anxiety in the same 20 clients at intake, after 6 weeks and after 12 weeks. If the spread of the intake to 6 week changes is very different from the spread of the 6 to 12 week changes, sphericity is violated. Option b is right because sphericity is exactly the equal-variance condition on difference scores within the same people. Exam trap: Levene's test also concerns equal variances, so it tempts, but it compares separate, independent groups, not repeated scores from the same individuals.", "traps": {"a": "Levene's test (Howard Levene, 1960) checks homogeneity of variance: whether independent groups have equal variances, as the independent t test and between-groups ANOVA assume. It does not deal with paired scores from the same people.", "c": "The independence assumption says each observation is unrelated to the others, secured by random sampling and assignment. Repeated measures deliberately break independence across conditions, which is why sphericity is needed instead.", "d": "'Pairs assumption' is not a recognised statistical term. It simply borrows the word 'pairs' from the stem to look plausible."}}, {"q": "The Weber fraction for certain sensory dimensions is given below. Indicate the most sensitive dimension:", "concept": "The answer is d: the dimension with the Weber fraction 0.013 is the most sensitive. Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834) found that the just noticeable difference (JND), the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect half the time, is not a fixed amount but a constant fraction of the starting (standard) stimulus. Weber's law is written delta I / I = k, where I is the standard intensity, delta I is the JND and k is the Weber fraction (Weber's constant) for that dimension. The fraction differs from one sensory dimension to another but stays roughly constant within a dimension across the middle range of intensities. Because k is the proportion by which a stimulus must change before the change is noticed, a smaller fraction means a smaller change is enough, so the sense is more finely tuned. A fraction of 0.013 means a change of only 1.3 per cent is noticed; 0.083 means a change of 8.3 per cent is needed. Gustav Theodor Fechner (1860) built on this to derive his logarithmic law, S = k log I, and founded psychophysics. Example: starting from a standard of 100 units, Arjun detects a change of only 1.3 units on a dimension with k = 0.013, but on a dimension with k = 0.083 he needs a change of 8.3 units before he notices anything. Option d is right because 0.013 is the smallest fraction, so the smallest proportional change is detected. Exam trap: students assume a bigger number means a stronger sense and pick 0.083; in Weber's law, the bigger the fraction, the poorer the discrimination.", "traps": {"a": "0.083 is the largest fraction listed: the stimulus must change by about 8 per cent before the change is noticed, so this is the least sensitive dimension, not the most.", "b": "0.048 needs a change of about 5 per cent to be detected. It is finer than 0.083 but coarser than 0.036 and 0.013.", "c": "0.036 needs a change of about 3.6 per cent. It is the second smallest fraction, so the dimension is fairly sensitive, but 0.013 is finer still."}}, {"q": "The intelligence test that embodies two theoretical models of intelligence, viz, the approach of Luria and the CHC model is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children, Second Edition (KABC-II), by Alan Kaufman and Nadeen Kaufman (2004), is built on two theoretical models of intelligence at once. The original K-ABC (1983) rested on the neuropsychological model of Alexander Luria (1973), especially sequential processing (handling information in serial order, as in repeating a digit string) and simultaneous processing (integrating many pieces at once, as in completing a picture). The KABC-II, for ages 3 to 18, keeps a Luria model with four scales (Sequential, Simultaneous, Learning and Planning), which yields the Mental Processing Index. It also offers a Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which reads the same scales as short-term memory (Gsm), visual processing (Gv), long-term storage and retrieval (Glr) and fluid reasoning (Gf), and adds a Knowledge scale for crystallised ability (Gc), yielding the Fluid-Crystallized Index. The examiner chooses the model before testing: the Luria model is preferred when a child's language or cultural background would make knowledge-based items unfair, the CHC model when the child shares the mainstream language and culture. A Nonverbal Index is also available. Example: for Arjun, whose home language differs from the language of testing, the psychologist picks the Luria model so that vocabulary and general knowledge do not pull his score down. Option a is right because the KABC-II is the only test listed that is explicitly built on both Luria's model and CHC theory. Exam trap: the SB5 also draws on CHC theory, so it tempts, but it uses CHC alone, with no Luria framework.", "traps": {"b": "The Detroit Tests of Learning Aptitude, Fourth Edition (Donald Hammill, 1998) is a battery of aptitude subtests for ages 6 to 17, grouped into composites such as verbal and non-verbal or attention-enhanced and attention-reduced. It is not built on Luria plus CHC.", "c": "The Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition (Gale Roid, 2003) measures five CHC-derived factors (fluid reasoning, knowledge, quantitative reasoning, visual-spatial processing and working memory) in verbal and non-verbal domains. It rests on CHC only, not on Luria.", "d": "The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children grew from David Wechsler's (1949) global view of intelligence; later editions realigned their index scores towards CHC factors. It does not offer a Luria-based model."}}, {"q": "The term double depression is used to describe:", "concept": "The answer is b: double depression means a major depressive episode superimposed on dysthymia. Dysthymia (now persistent depressive disorder in DSM-5) is a chronic, milder depression: depressed mood on most days for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children and adolescents), with two or more symptoms such as poor appetite, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or hopelessness, and never more than 2 months free of symptoms. When a full major depressive episode develops on top of this long-standing low mood, the person has two depressions at once. Martin Keller and Robert Shapiro (1982) coined the term. Follow-up studies by Martin Keller and colleagues showed that such patients often recover from the major episode fairly quickly but fall back to their dysthymic baseline rather than to full health, and they relapse more often, so the outlook is worse than for major depression alone. DSM-5 folds the pattern into persistent depressive disorder, using the specifier 'with intermittent major depressive episodes' or 'with persistent major depressive episode'. Treatment usually combines antidepressants with psychotherapy, such as the Cognitive Behavioral Analysis System of Psychotherapy (CBASP) of James McCullough (2000), designed for chronic depression. Example: Meera has felt low and joyless for four years but kept working; after losing her job she develops severe depression with insomnia and suicidal thoughts. She has double depression. Option b is right because the term names exactly this layering of a major episode on chronic dysthymia. Exam trap: 'a severe bout of major depression' sounds double in strength, but the term refers to two disorders together, not to severity.", "traps": {"a": "A severe bout of major depression is simply a major depressive episode with the 'severe' specifier. Severity alone does not make it double; there must be underlying dysthymia.", "c": "Repeated major episodes describe recurrent major depressive disorder, and DSM requires at least 2 months between episodes for them to count as separate. That is recurrence, not double depression.", "d": "A major depressive episode caused by a medical illness is depressive disorder due to another medical condition. Double depression involves dysthymia, not a medical condition."}}, {"q": "The set of ethical guidelines that researchers in Psychology must adhere to while conducting studies on human participants is provided by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the ethical guidelines psychologists must follow when they study human participants are set out in the APA ethics code, the Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association. The first APA code appeared in 1953; the current version dates from 2002, with amendments in 2010 and 2016. It has five general principles (beneficence and nonmaleficence; fidelity and responsibility; integrity; justice; respect for people's rights and dignity) and a set of enforceable standards. The research standards cover approval by an institutional review board, informed consent (including the right to withdraw without penalty), the narrow cases where consent may be waived, inducements, deception only when the study's value justifies it and no non-deceptive method exists, prompt debriefing, humane care of animals, honest reporting and no plagiarism; privacy and confidentiality have their own section. Other bodies have similar codes, such as the British Psychological Society, and Indian universities use institutional ethics committees that apply the same principles. Example: before Kabir interviews college students about self-harm, the APA code requires him to obtain ethics committee approval, explain the study and the right to stop at any time, protect the students' identities, and give helpline numbers during debriefing. Option b is right because the APA code is the professional code written specifically for psychologists, including their research with people. Exam trap: the Belmont Report also concerns research ethics and is often quoted in psychology texts, but it is a US government report for all human research, not psychology's own code.", "traps": {"a": "The Nuremberg Code (1947) came out of the trial of Nazi doctors and set ten principles for medical experiments, above all voluntary consent. It is a landmark in research ethics but not the code psychologists follow.", "c": "The Belmont Report (1979), from the US National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects, set out respect for persons, beneficence and justice for all federally funded research with people. It is a broad regulatory framework, not psychology's own professional code.", "d": "The M'Naghten Rule (1843), from the trial of Daniel M'Naghten, is a legal test of insanity: the accused did not know the nature of the act or that it was wrong. It belongs to criminal law, not research ethics."}}, {"q": "In an ROC curve, the greater the d' value and more bowed the curve, then:", "concept": "The answer is b: the greater the d' and the more bowed the ROC curve, the higher the hit rate and the lower the false alarm rate. Signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966) treats detection as a decision under uncertainty. On each trial there is either noise alone or a signal added to noise, and the internal evidence in each case forms an overlapping normal distribution. d' (d prime) is the sensitivity index: the distance between the means of the noise and signal-plus-noise distributions, in standard deviation units. The observer also sets a criterion (beta), saying yes whenever the evidence is above it. Four outcomes follow: hit (yes to a signal), miss (no to a signal), false alarm (yes to noise) and correct rejection (no to noise). The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve plots the hit rate against the false alarm rate as the criterion moves from strict to lax. If d' is 0, the two distributions coincide and the curve is the diagonal: hits rise only as fast as false alarms, which is chance. As d' grows, the distributions separate and the curve bows towards the top-left corner, so at any false alarm rate the hit rate is higher. Example: Riya, an experienced radiologist, has a larger d' for spotting tumours than a trainee; her ROC curve bows further, so she catches more tumours while raising fewer false alarms. Option b is right because a more bowed curve means more hits for fewer false alarms. Exam trap: shifting the criterion moves hits and false alarms together along one curve; only a greater d' lifts the whole curve towards the top-left.", "traps": {"a": "Higher false alarms with lower hits would put the curve below the diagonal, worse than chance. That is the opposite of what a large d' produces.", "c": "Misses and hits together make up all signal trials, so more misses means fewer hits. A large d' reduces misses, so this option gets the signal side backwards.", "d": "Correct rejections and false alarms together make up all noise trials, so both cannot rise at once. A larger d' raises correct rejections while lowering false alarms."}}, {"q": "When a researcher sets a significance level (alpha level), p < 0.05 changed to a more stringent level p <= 0.01 then:", "concept": "The answer is b: making alpha stricter, from 0.05 to 0.01, lowers the risk of a Type I error but raises the risk of a Type II error. In significance testing, a Type I error (alpha error) is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true, a false positive. A Type II error (beta error) is retaining a null hypothesis that is actually false, a real effect missed. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) built this two-error framework; Ronald Fisher (1925) had popularised 0.05 as a convenient cut-off. Alpha is chosen by the researcher and is exactly the probability of a Type I error. Beta is not chosen; it follows from sample size, effect size, variability and alpha itself. Power is 1 minus beta, the chance of detecting a real effect. Suppose Arjun tests whether a new relaxation programme lowers exam anxiety. At alpha 0.05 (two-tailed) his z must pass 1.96; at 0.01 it must pass 2.58. The rejection region shrinks, so he will rarely declare a false effect, but a real, modest effect now more often falls short of the cut-off and is missed. When only alpha moves, the two errors trade off like a seesaw. Option b is right because tightening alpha reduces Type I errors and, with sample and effect unchanged, increases Type II errors and lowers power. The only way to lower both at once is to add information: a larger sample, a stronger manipulation or less measurement noise. Exam trap: examiners reverse the seesaw (option d describes loosening alpha to 0.10) or offer 'both lower', which needs a bigger sample, not a stricter alpha.", "traps": {"a": "Both errors rising would need two changes at once, for example a looser alpha together with a much smaller sample. A stricter alpha by definition shrinks the Type I risk, because alpha is that risk.", "c": "Lowering both errors together is possible only by adding information (a larger sample, a bigger effect or less error variance). Changing alpha alone simply moves risk from one error to the other.", "d": "This is the pattern for loosening alpha, say from 0.05 to 0.10: a wider rejection region gives more false positives but fewer misses. It is the exact reverse of what the stem describes."}}, {"q": "A component of the extrapyramidal system that plays an inhibitory role in spontaneous motor activity:", "concept": "The answer is a: the caudate nucleus is the extrapyramidal structure classically described as inhibiting spontaneous motor activity. The pyramidal system is the corticospinal tract, running from the motor cortex through the medullary pyramids to the spinal cord. The extrapyramidal system is the older label for the other motor structures, chiefly the basal ganglia: the caudate nucleus and putamen (together the striatum, which receives cortical input), the globus pallidus, and their partners the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra. These nuclei do not command muscles; they modulate the cortex through the thalamus, selecting some movements and suppressing others. Classic physiological psychology texts give the caudate a braking role. Electrical stimulation of the caudate in animals such as cats halts ongoing movement, the 'caudate arrest' response: an animal that is walking or eating simply stops. Caudate lesions do the opposite, giving restless hyperactivity and 'obstinate progression', the animal pressing forward against a wall. In Huntington's disease striatal neurons die, the caudate first, and unwanted dance-like movements (chorea) are released, which fits the same idea of lost inhibition. Option a is right because stimulation, lesion and clinical evidence all point to the caudate as the brake on spontaneous activity in the older extrapyramidal account the stem uses. Exam trap: the cerebellum is often slipped in as a motor structure, but it coordinates and times movement and is not part of the extrapyramidal system proper. Key note: in the modern circuit model (Garrett Alexander, Mahlon DeLong and Peter Strick, 1986; Roger Albin, Anne Young and John Penney, 1989), the internal globus pallidus is the GABAergic output that holds the thalamus in check, so some defend b. The stem's wording about spontaneous motor activity follows the older caudate-inhibition account, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The globus pallidus is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia; in the modern circuit its internal segment tonically inhibits the thalamus, which is why it tempts. But the classic description of a brake on spontaneous activity, with arrest on stimulation and hyperactivity after lesions, belongs to the caudate.", "c": "The putamen joins the caudate in the striatum and is most tied to the motor cortex loop that starts and runs body movements. Textbooks do not single it out as the inhibitor of spontaneous activity.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates the timing, force and accuracy of movement and supports motor learning; its damage gives ataxia and intention tremor, not released activity. It is not counted in the extrapyramidal system proper."}}, {"q": "The Rescorla-Wagner model was developed to explain the phenomena of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Rescorla-Wagner model was developed to explain blocking. Before the late 1960s, conditioning was thought to need only contiguity, the conditioned stimulus (CS) and unconditioned stimulus (US) close together in time. Robert Rescorla (1968) showed that rats did not fear a tone if shocks were just as likely without it: what matters is contingency, how well the CS predicts the US. Leon Kamin (1969) then found blocking. Rats first learned that a noise predicted shock; then a noise plus light compound was followed by the same shock. Tested alone, the light produced almost no fear, although it had been paired with shock many times. Contiguity could not explain this. Robert Rescorla and Allan Wagner (1972) proposed that an animal learns only when surprised. On each trial the change in a CS's associative strength is proportional to the prediction error: the most the US can support (lambda) minus the summed strength of all stimuli present, scaled by salience and learning rate. In blocking the noise already predicts the shock fully, so the error is zero and the light gains nothing. Imagine Kabir's dog already salivating to a bell; adding a lamp to the bell before food teaches the dog nothing about the lamp. Option c is right because blocking was the puzzle the model was built to solve; it also predicts overshadowing, conditioned inhibition and overexpectation. It fails on latent inhibition (Robert Lubow and A. U. Moore, 1959), slower learning after unreinforced pre-exposure. Exam trap: 'contiguity' tempts because it is a classic conditioning term, but it is the old account the model replaced with surprise.", "traps": {"a": "Second-order conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), uses an established CS in place of the US to condition a new stimulus. It was not the model's target, and the basic model even predicts that the new stimulus should become inhibitory.", "b": "Autoshaping, found by Paul Brown and Herbert Jenkins (1968), is pigeons pecking a lit key that merely precedes food, though pecking is never required. It shows Pavlovian sign-tracking, not cue competition, so it was not what the model was built for.", "d": "Contiguity, the view of Ivan Pavlov and Edwin Guthrie that pairing in time is enough for learning, is the older account. Blocking is precisely a case where contiguity is present but learning fails, so the model replaced it with prediction error."}}, {"q": "The part of the brain mediating the analgesic effects of opiate drugs:", "concept": "The answer is b: the periaqueductal grey (PAG) of the midbrain mediates much of the pain-relieving effect of opiate drugs such as morphine. The PAG is the grey matter surrounding the cerebral aqueduct, the narrow canal through the midbrain. Three lines of evidence tied analgesia to it. First, David Reynolds (1969) electrically stimulated the PAG in rats and could operate on their abdomens without anaesthetic, a finding called stimulation-produced analgesia. Second, Candace Pert and Solomon Snyder (1973) demonstrated opiate receptors in the brain, dense in the PAG, and John Hughes and Hans Kosterlitz (1975) isolated the enkephalins, the brain's own opioids. Third, tiny injections of morphine into the PAG relieve pain, while blocking opioid receptors there weakens the effect of morphine given to the whole body. The circuit, set out by Allan Basbaum and Howard Fields (1978), descends from the PAG to the serotonergic raphe nuclei of the medulla and then to the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, where interneurons release enkephalin and block incoming pain signals before they rise to the brain. When Riya is given morphine after an appendix operation, part of her relief comes from switching on this descending brake. Option b is right because the PAG is rich in opioid receptors and is the starting point of the descending pain-control pathway through which opiates act. Exam trap: the substantia nigra and red nucleus also lie in the midbrain, so remembering only 'a midbrain structure' can mislead; and the thalamus relays pain upward rather than suppressing it.", "traps": {"a": "The red nucleus is a motor nucleus of the midbrain tegmentum and the source of the rubrospinal tract, which helps control limb movements. It plays no central part in opiate analgesia.", "c": "The substantia nigra holds the dopamine neurons of the nigrostriatal pathway to the caudate and putamen, which die in Parkinson's disease. It concerns movement, not opioid pain control.", "d": "The thalamus is the sensory relay; its ventral posterior nuclei carry pain signals up to the somatosensory cortex. It transmits pain rather than being the main site where opiates suppress it."}}, {"q": "X-linked recessive conditions associated with mental retardation include all the following, EXCEPT:", "concept": "The answer is d: cri-du-chat syndrome is not an X-linked recessive condition; it is an autosomal chromosomal deletion. In X-linked recessive inheritance the faulty gene lies on the X chromosome. A female with one faulty copy is usually a healthy carrier, because her second X masks it; a male has only one X, so a single faulty copy produces the disorder. Such conditions therefore strike mainly boys and pass through carrier mothers. If Meera carries a faulty X-linked gene, each son has a 50% chance of being affected and each daughter a 50% chance of being a carrier. Three of the options follow this pattern and can impair intellect. Duchenne muscular dystrophy comes from mutations in the dystrophin gene on the X; boys develop progressive muscle weakness, and about a third also have cognitive impairment. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, described by Michael Lesch and William Nyhan (1964), is a deficiency of the enzyme HPRT, causing excess uric acid, dystonia, intellectual disability and striking self-biting. X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy comes from ABCD1 mutations; very long chain fatty acids build up, and the childhood cerebral form brings behavioural and cognitive decline as myelin breaks down. Cri-du-chat syndrome, identified by Jerome Lejeune (1963), is a deletion on the short arm of chromosome 5 (5p minus), so it affects both sexes; infants have a high, cat-like cry, a small head and severe intellectual disability. Option d is right because its cause lies on an autosome, not on the X. Exam trap: 'genetic' is not the same as 'X-linked'; deletion syndromes (cri-du-chat, Williams, Prader-Willi) are chromosomal errors, whereas fragile X, Lesch-Nyhan and Duchenne sit on the X.", "traps": {"a": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked recessive (dystrophin gene) and affects mainly boys, with cognitive impairment in about a third. It fits the list, so it cannot be the exception.", "b": "Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (Michael Lesch and William Nyhan, 1964) is X-linked recessive HPRT deficiency with intellectual disability and self-injury. It belongs in the list.", "c": "Adrenoleukodystrophy in its common form is X-linked (ABCD1 gene), and the childhood cerebral form causes cognitive and behavioural decline. It belongs in the list, even though its name hides the X link."}}, {"q": "Ability to repeat spoken words is preserved in:", "concept": "The answer is c: repetition of spoken words is preserved in transcortical motor aphasia. Aphasia is a language disorder after brain damage, usually to the left hemisphere. Carl Wernicke (1874) and Ludwig Lichtheim (1885) classified the aphasias by three tests: fluency of spontaneous speech, comprehension, and repetition. Repetition depends on the perisylvian loop: Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus takes in the heard words, the arcuate fasciculus carries them forward, and Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus speaks them. Any lesion on this loop spoils repetition. The transcortical aphasias come from damage in the border (watershed) zones around the loop, which cut it off from the rest of the cortex but leave it intact, so repetition survives. In transcortical motor aphasia the lesion lies in front of or above Broca's area, often in the supplementary motor area or a watershed infarct between the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. Spontaneous speech is sparse and effortful, with long delays, but comprehension is fair and repetition is good. Arjun, after a stroke of this kind, barely starts a sentence on his own, yet repeats 'the train leaves Chennai at nine' word for word. Transcortical sensory aphasia is the posterior mirror image: fluent speech, poor comprehension, preserved repetition, often with echolalia. Option c is right because the perisylvian loop is spared, so the patient can echo words he cannot produce spontaneously. Exam trap: conduction aphasia sounds like a 'repetition' answer, but it is the aphasia defined by very poor repetition with good comprehension.", "traps": {"a": "Motor (Broca's) aphasia, from damage to Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Paul Broca, 1861), gives non-fluent, telegraphic speech with fairly good comprehension. The lesion sits on the repetition loop, so repetition is poor.", "b": "Sensory (Wernicke's) aphasia, from damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus (Carl Wernicke, 1874), gives fluent but empty speech and poor comprehension. The patient cannot process the words heard, so repetition fails.", "d": "Conduction aphasia follows damage to the arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal gyrus, cutting the link between Wernicke's and Broca's areas. Speech is fluent and comprehension good, but repetition is its defining failure."}}, {"q": "In the construction of a psychological test, for the sharpest discrimination among examinees, the item index difficulty should be set at:", "concept": "The answer is b: an item difficulty of 50% gives the sharpest discrimination among examinees. The item difficulty index (p) is simply the proportion of examinees who pass an item, so a high p means an easy item: 80% passing is easy, 40% passing is hard. An item passed by everyone or by no one tells nobody apart and adds nothing to the test. Each item discriminates by separating those who pass it from those who fail it. With 100 examinees, an item passed by 50 separates 50 times 50, or 2,500 pairs of people; one passed by 80 separates only 80 times 20, or 1,600 pairs. Statistically, the variance of a pass-fail item is p times q (q is 1 minus p), and it peaks at p = 0.50, where it equals 0.25. Because the discrimination index cannot be high when p is near 0 or 1, items near 0.50 also allow the highest discrimination. So when Meera builds a 40-item aptitude test, she keeps items whose difficulty clusters around 0.50, perhaps spread from 0.30 to 0.70 so the whole ability range is covered. Guessing shifts the ideal a little: for multiple-choice items the best p lies midway between the chance score and 1.00, about 0.62 for four options. Mastery and screening tests deliberately use easier items, since they only need to pick out a small failing group. Option b is right because a 50% pass rate maximises item variance and the number of pass-fail distinctions. Exam trap: examiners count on confusing the index with hardness; a 'high' difficulty index such as 80% means an easy item, not a hard one.", "traps": {"a": "At 40% the item is harder than ideal; it still discriminates fairly well, but its variance (0.24) and its number of pass-fail pairs (2,400 in 100 examinees) fall below the maximum reached at 50%.", "c": "60% is close to the guessing-adjusted optimum for four-option multiple-choice items, which is why it tempts. The stem asks for the general rule for sharpest discrimination, the point of maximum variance, which is 50%.", "d": "An 80% item is easy; it separates only the few who fail from everyone else (1,600 pairs in 100 examinees). Such items suit mastery or screening tests, not maximum discrimination."}}, {"q": "A lesion at the optic chiasma in the visual pathway results in:", "concept": "The answer is b: a lesion at the optic chiasm causes bitemporal hemianopia, loss of the outer (temporal) half of the visual field in each eye. Each retina has a nasal half (towards the nose) and a temporal half (towards the temple). Because the lens inverts the image, the nasal half-retina receives light from the temporal (outer) visual field. At the optic chiasm, just below the hypothalamus and above the pituitary gland, fibres from the two nasal half-retinas cross to the opposite side, while temporal fibres stay on their own side. This partial crossing sends the whole left visual field to the right hemisphere and the right field to the left. A lesion pressing on the middle of the chiasm cuts only the crossing nasal fibres, so both temporal fields are lost and the patient keeps a central, tunnel-like band of vision. The classic cause is a pituitary adenoma growing upwards. Kabir, a 45-year-old clerk, keeps bumping into people on both sides and has stopped driving because he cannot see cars beside him; an MRI shows a pituitary tumour. Lesions behind the chiasm (optic tract, optic radiations or primary visual cortex) give a homonymous hemianopia, the same half of the field lost in both eyes, while a lesion of one optic nerve blinds one eye. Option b is right because the chiasm is the one place where the fibres serving both temporal fields come together. Exam trap: remember 'nasal fibres cross, temporal fields are lost'; mixing up retinal halves with field halves leads students to nasal or homonymous losses.", "traps": {"a": "Cortical blindness follows bilateral damage to the primary visual cortex (V1) of the occipital lobes, with normal pupil reactions because the pathway to the midbrain is intact. A single midline chiasm lesion cannot remove all vision.", "c": "Homonymous hemianopia, the same half-field lost in both eyes, comes from a one-sided lesion behind the chiasm, in the optic tract, optic radiation or occipital cortex. At the chiasm the loss is heteronymous, opposite halves in the two eyes.", "d": "Quadrantanopia, loss of a quarter of the field, comes from partial damage to the optic radiations: Meyer's loop in the temporal lobe gives an upper quadrant loss ('pie in the sky'), parietal fibres a lower one. It is a sign of damage behind the chiasm."}}, {"q": "Narcolepsy is characterised by: 1. Hypnagogic hallucinations 2. Repeated irresistible attacks of refreshing sleep 3. Cataplexy 4. Prolonged, deep nocturnal sleep. Choose the correct option:", "concept": "The answer is a: narcolepsy is characterised by hypnagogic hallucinations, repeated irresistible attacks of refreshing sleep, and cataplexy, so 1, 2 and 3 are correct. Narcolepsy, named by Jean-Baptiste Gelineau (1880), is a disorder in which REM sleep intrudes into waking. Its classic tetrad is excessive daytime sleepiness with sleep attacks, cataplexy, hypnagogic (falling asleep) or hypnopompic (waking) hallucinations, and sleep paralysis. Sleep attacks are irresistible, often brief, and leave the person refreshed for a while. Cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion, especially laughter, with full consciousness: REM atonia breaking into wakefulness. The hallucinations and sleep paralysis are REM dream imagery and REM paralysis at the edges of sleep. Recordings show sleep-onset REM periods, REM within minutes of falling asleep. The cause of type 1 narcolepsy is loss of the orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus: Emmanuel Mignot and colleagues (1999) traced narcolepsy in dogs to a faulty hypocretin receptor gene, and in 2000 his group and others showed that human patients have lost these neurons. Riya, a college student, falls asleep in every afternoon lecture, wakes from a 15 minute nap feeling clear, and her knees buckle when her friends make her laugh. Statement 4 is false. Night sleep in narcolepsy is not prolonged and deep; it is fragmented, with frequent awakenings and vivid dreams. Total sleep over 24 hours is roughly normal; it is simply badly placed. Option a is right because it holds the three true features and leaves out the false one. Exam trap: long, deep night sleep with unrefreshing naps and 'sleep drunkenness' on waking describes idiopathic hypersomnia, the look-alike borrowed for statement 4.", "traps": {"b": "This combination keeps statement 4, prolonged deep night sleep, which is false because narcoleptic night sleep is fragmented. It also drops cataplexy, the most specific sign of the disorder.", "c": "This drops hypnagogic hallucinations, a true tetrad feature, and adds the false statement 4 about prolonged deep night sleep.", "d": "This drops the irresistible refreshing sleep attacks, the core symptom, and adds the false statement 4; long deep night sleep fits idiopathic hypersomnia, not narcolepsy."}}, {"q": "A Posterior pituitary hormone:", "concept": "The answer is c: oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone. The pituitary has two parts with different origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is true glandular tissue. It makes its own hormones, growth hormone, prolactin, thyroid-stimulating hormone (thyrotropin), adrenocorticotropic hormone and the gonadotropins FSH and LH, under the control of releasing and inhibiting hormones that the hypothalamus sends down a local portal blood system. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is neural tissue, an outgrowth of the hypothalamus, and makes nothing itself. Oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) are synthesised in neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, carried down their axons, stored in the posterior pituitary and released into the blood. Oxytocin contracts the uterus in labour and squeezes milk out of the breast during suckling (the let-down reflex); it also shapes bonding and trust. Vasopressin makes the kidneys retain water. When Meera nurses her newborn, prolactin keeps her milk supply going, while each feed triggers an oxytocin surge that lets the milk flow. Option c is right because oxytocin is one of only two hormones released from the posterior lobe. Exam trap: prolactin and oxytocin both concern breastfeeding, so they are swapped; prolactin (anterior pituitary) makes milk, oxytocin (posterior pituitary) ejects it. A second trap is thyrotropin versus thyrotropin-releasing hormone: TSH comes from the anterior pituitary, TRH from the hypothalamus.", "traps": {"a": "Prolactin is made by lactotroph cells of the anterior pituitary and stimulates milk production; hypothalamic dopamine normally holds it down. It is not a posterior pituitary hormone.", "b": "Somatostatin is a hypothalamic inhibiting hormone that stops anterior pituitary release of growth hormone (and TSH), and it is also made by delta cells of the pancreas. It acts on the pituitary rather than being released from its posterior lobe.", "d": "Thyrotropin (thyroid-stimulating hormone, TSH) is an anterior pituitary hormone released in response to hypothalamic TRH; it drives the thyroid to make thyroxine."}}, {"q": "The non-parametric equivalent of the ANOVA test is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Kruskal-Wallis H test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA. Parametric tests such as the t test and ANOVA compare means and assume interval or ratio scores, roughly normal distributions and equal variances. When those assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or badly skewed scores, researchers turn to non-parametric (distribution-free) tests, which work on ranks. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) extended the rank-sum logic of the Mann-Whitney U test to three or more independent groups. All scores from all groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest; the mean rank of each group is compared with the overall mean rank, and the resulting H statistic is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom (k is the number of groups). A significant H, like a significant F, says only that at least one group differs, so pairwise follow-up tests are needed. Suppose Meera compares pain ratings on a 1 to 5 scale from three groups of patients given yoga, physiotherapy or medication alone. The ratings are ordinal and skewed, so instead of a one-way ANOVA she runs a Kruskal-Wallis test. Learn the whole matching table: independent t test to Mann-Whitney U; paired t test to Wilcoxon signed-rank; one-way ANOVA to Kruskal-Wallis H; repeated-measures ANOVA to the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937); Pearson's r to Spearman's rho or Kendall's tau. Option c is right because Kruskal-Wallis, like one-way ANOVA, compares three or more independent groups, using ranks instead of means. Exam trap: Mann-Whitney U is the best-known rank test and is literally the two-group case of Kruskal-Wallis, so it tempts, but its parametric partner is the independent t test.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares only two independent groups by ranks, so its parametric partner is the independent t test, not ANOVA.", "b": "The runs test (the Wald-Wolfowitz test of Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz, 1940) checks whether a sequence is random, or whether two samples come from the same distribution, by counting runs of like values. It does not compare several groups.", "d": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) ranks the differences within pairs of related scores, so it replaces the paired t test. It handles two related conditions, not several groups."}}, {"q": "Match List I (Disorder) with List II (Symptoms): a. Generalized Anxiety disorder, b. Dementia, c. Schizophrenia, d. Complex partial. 1. Insidious or sudden onset, 2. Deja vu, 3. Elevated blink rate, 4. Persistent worry", "concept": "The answer is b: a-4, b-1, c-3, d-2, pairing generalised anxiety disorder with persistent worry, dementia with insidious or sudden onset, schizophrenia with an elevated blink rate, and complex partial seizures with deja vu. Generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is defined by persistent, excessive worry about many everyday matters that is hard to control, on more days than not for at least 6 months in DSM-5, with restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension or poor sleep. Dementia is an acquired decline in memory and other cognitive abilities that disrupts daily life, and its onset depends on the cause. Alzheimer's disease begins insidiously and progresses gradually, whereas vascular dementia often starts suddenly after a stroke and worsens stepwise, so 'insidious or sudden onset' covers the dementias as a group. In schizophrenia, the spontaneous eye blink rate is raised in many unmedicated patients; because blinking rises with central dopamine activity, studies from the late 1970s onward read it as a sign of dopamine overactivity, and it tends to fall with antipsychotic treatment. Complex partial seizures (now called focal impaired awareness seizures) usually arise in the temporal lobe. They often begin with an aura such as deja vu, the feeling that a new moment has been lived before, or a rising feeling in the stomach; then the person stares, loses awareness and shows automatisms such as lip smacking. Kabir, 30, suddenly feels he has lived this exact moment before, then stares blankly and fumbles with his shirt for a minute, remembering nothing afterwards. Option b is right because it is the only option that gets all four pairs. Exam trap: deja vu tempts as an odd experience for schizophrenia, but as a brief, stereotyped aura it points to temporal lobe epilepsy.", "traps": {"a": "This keeps b-1 and d-2 but swaps the other two: an elevated blink rate is not a GAD sign (a-3), and persistent worry is the core of GAD, not of schizophrenia (c-4).", "c": "Only b-1 is right here: the blink rate belongs to schizophrenia, not GAD (a-3); deja vu is the temporal lobe seizure aura, not a schizophrenia sign (c-2); and persistent worry is GAD, not complex partial seizures (d-4).", "d": "This gets a-4 and b-1 but swaps the last two: deja vu belongs to complex partial seizures, not schizophrenia (c-2), and the elevated blink rate belongs to schizophrenia, not epilepsy (d-3)."}}, {"q": "The fractional antedating goal response proposed by Hull is roughly equivalent to:", "concept": "The answer is a: Clark Hull's fractional antedating (anticipatory) goal response is roughly equivalent to expectancy. Clark Hull (1943) built a mechanistic stimulus-response theory of learning on drive reduction, set out in Principles of Behavior. He still had to explain behaviour that looks purposeful, as when a rat runs eagerly toward a goal box it cannot yet see. In papers of 1930 and 1931 he proposed the fractional antedating goal response, written rg. When a hungry rat eats in the goal box, the full goal response (chewing, salivating, licking) is conditioned to the cues there. Because cues in the alley resemble goal box cues, small fragments of the goal response, such as tiny chewing or licking movements, become conditioned to them and move forward in time, occurring before the goal is reached. Each fragment produces its own internal stimulus, sg, and this rg-sg mechanism links the chain of responses and keeps the animal directed toward the goal. In effect the rat anticipates food without Hull having to speak of mental states. Arjun, walking to the hostel canteen at lunchtime, starts salivating and speeds up before he sees the food; in Hull's terms, his rg-sg is pulling him along. Edward Tolman (1932) explained the same purposive behaviour with expectancies, cognitive beliefs that a response will lead to a goal. Hull's rg is often called his stimulus-response translation of Tolman's expectancy. Option a is right because rg is Hull's mechanistic stand-in for an animal's expectation of the goal. Exam trap: 'fractional' sounds like part-learning or step-by-step learning, so trial and error is chosen, but rg concerns anticipation of the goal, not how a response is first found.", "traps": {"b": "Trial and error learning is Edward Thorndike's (1898) account of cats in puzzle boxes stumbling on the escape response, which the law of effect then stamps in. It describes how a response is found, not anticipation of the goal.", "c": "Insight is Wolfgang Kohler's (1925) Gestalt account of chimpanzees suddenly restructuring a problem, as in stacking boxes to reach bananas. It is a cognitive leap, not a conditioned fragment of the goal response.", "d": "Solution learning is O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor term for instrumental learning of responses that solve a problem, set against sign learning (classical conditioning of emotions). It is not Hull's anticipatory mechanism."}}, {"q": "The interactive and independent effects of two or more independent variables on the dependent variable is studied using:", "concept": "The answer is b: a factorial design studies both the independent (main) effects and the interactive effects of two or more independent variables on a dependent variable. In a factorial design every level of each independent variable (factor) is combined with every level of the others, so the conditions form a grid of cells. Ronald Fisher (1935) promoted the approach in The Design of Experiments, arguing that varying several factors together is more efficient and more informative than studying one factor at a time. A design with two factors of two levels each is called a 2 x 2 design and has four cells; a 2 x 3 design has six. The analysis, usually a factorial ANOVA, gives a main effect for each factor, the effect of that factor averaged over the levels of the others, and an interaction, which exists when the effect of one factor depends on the level of another. Suppose Kabir studies exam performance with two factors: type of revision (spaced or massed) and anxiety level (high or low). He might find a main effect of revision (spaced is better overall), a main effect of anxiety, and an interaction: spacing helps the low-anxiety students much more than the high-anxiety ones. Plotted, the lines for the two anxiety groups are not parallel, the visual sign of an interaction. Only a factorial arrangement can reveal such a dependency. Option b is right because the factorial design is the one built to estimate the independent and interactive effects of several independent variables together. Exam trap: within-groups and matched-groups designs describe how participants are assigned, not how many factors are crossed; a factorial design can itself be between, within or mixed.", "traps": {"a": "A within-groups (repeated-measures) design puts every participant through all conditions, controlling individual differences. It concerns how participants are assigned and can study a single independent variable, so it does not by itself test interactions.", "c": "A matched-groups design pairs participants on a relevant variable (such as IQ) and then splits each pair between conditions. It is a control technique for one comparison, not a way of crossing several factors.", "d": "Pre-experimental designs in the scheme of Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), such as the one-shot case study and the one-group pretest-posttest design, lack proper control groups. They cannot isolate even one effect cleanly, let alone interactions."}}, {"q": "Which theory of emotion contends that arousal and appraisal are independent processes brought about by the action of the thalamus?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Cannon-Bard theory holds that arousal and the experience of emotion are independent, simultaneous processes triggered by the thalamus. Walter Cannon (1927) and his student Philip Bard (1928) attacked the earlier James-Lange theory, which said that bodily arousal comes first and the felt emotion is our perception of it (we are afraid because we tremble). Cannon raised several objections: animals whose viscera were surgically cut off from the brain still showed emotional behaviour; the same visceral changes occur in fear, anger, fever and exercise, so they cannot tell emotions apart; the viscera respond too slowly to explain the speed of emotion; and injecting adrenaline produces arousal without a true emotion. In the Cannon-Bard view, an emotional stimulus is relayed through the thalamus, which sends signals at the same time upward to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling (the 'appraisal' of the stem), and downward to the body through the hypothalamus and autonomic nervous system, producing arousal. Neither causes the other. When Riya sees a snake on the path to her hostel, she feels fear and her heart races at the same instant, each from the thalamic signal. Bard's work on decorticate cats showed that 'sham rage' depended on the hypothalamus, which later shifted the centre downward, but the textbook theory still names the thalamus. Option b is right because only Cannon-Bard makes feeling and arousal independent and simultaneous via the thalamus. Exam trap: the Schachter-Singer theory also speaks of arousal and appraisal, but it makes them interact, with a cognitive label interpreting the arousal, rather than run independently.", "traps": {"a": "The James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885) makes bodily arousal come first and the emotion its perception, so the two are sequential and dependent, not independent. It gives the thalamus no special role.", "c": "The Schachter-Singer two-factor theory (Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer, 1962) says emotion is general arousal plus a cognitive label drawn from the situation, so the two factors interact. It is a cognitive theory, not a thalamic one.", "d": "Activation theory (Donald Lindsley, 1951) places emotion on a continuum of arousal produced by the brainstem reticular activating system. It concerns the level of arousal, not independent thalamic signals."}}, {"q": "An amnesic agent used in memory consolidation studies that produces a condition known as spreading depression:", "concept": "The answer is b: potassium chloride (KCl) applied to the cortex produces spreading depression and was used as an amnesic agent in memory consolidation studies. The consolidation hypothesis of Georg Elias Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) says a new memory stays fragile for a while before it becomes stable. Researchers tested it by giving an amnesic agent at different times after training: if a treatment given soon after learning wipes out the memory but the same treatment given hours later does not, the memory must have been consolidating in between. Several agents were used. Carl Duncan (1949) gave rats electroconvulsive shock and found such a retrograde gradient. Protein synthesis inhibitors such as puromycin and acetoxycycloheximide blocked the formation of long-term memory. Spreading depression works differently. Aristides Leao (1944) discovered spreading depression: a strong local stimulus (in later work usually concentrated KCl placed on the exposed cortex) sets off a wave of depolarisation that creeps across the cortex at a few millimetres per minute, silencing neural activity and flattening the EEG for minutes to hours. The cortex then recovers, so the method gives a reversible 'functional ablation'. Jan Bures and Olga Buresova (1960s) used it to depress one hemisphere of a rat during training or testing, showing that a memory could be confined to one side and studying how it transferred between hemispheres. Option b is right because KCl is the classic agent that produces cortical spreading depression. Exam trap: all four options disrupt memory, so the clue is the named condition: spreading depression means KCl, protein synthesis inhibition means puromycin or acetoxycycloheximide, and a seizure means ECS.", "traps": {"a": "Acetoxycycloheximide is a protein synthesis inhibitor, used by Bernard Agranoff and colleagues (1960s) in goldfish and by others in mice to block long-term memory formation. It acts on protein synthesis, not by spreading depression.", "c": "Puromycin, injected into the brains of mice by Louis and Josefa Flexner (1963), is another protein synthesis inhibitor that blocks consolidation. It does not set off a wave of cortical depolarisation.", "d": "Electroconvulsive shock produces a seizure that disrupts consolidation when given soon after training, the retrograde gradient of Carl Duncan (1949). Its effect comes from massive synchronous discharge, not from spreading depression."}}, {"q": "Suppose the reliability of an intelligence test is 0.40. How many times should the test be lengthened to reach a reliability coefficient of 0.80?", "concept": "The answer is d: the test must be made 6 times as long to raise its reliability from 0.40 to 0.80. Reliability rises with test length because random errors in individual items tend to cancel out as more items are added, while the true score part keeps adding up. Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910) independently published the formula that predicts this, now called the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula. In its general form, the new reliability equals k times r divided by [1 plus (k minus 1) times r], where r is the old reliability and k is the factor by which the length changes. Solving for k gives k = r_new (1 minus r_old) divided by [r_old (1 minus r_new)]. Here r_old is 0.40 and r_new is 0.80. The numerator is 0.80 x 0.60 = 0.48; the denominator is 0.40 x 0.20 = 0.08; so k = 0.48 / 0.08 = 6. Check by putting 6 back in: 6 x 0.40 = 2.4, and 1 + 5 x 0.40 = 3.0, so 2.4 / 3.0 = 0.80. If the original test had 20 items, it would need 120 comparable items. The formula assumes the added items are parallel to the old ones, measuring the same thing with similar difficulty and quality; adding poor or off-target items will not deliver the predicted gain. It also shows diminishing returns: each extra block of items adds less. Option d is right because only a sixfold lengthening brings 0.40 up to exactly 0.80. Exam trap: it feels natural that doubling a reliability needs a doubled test, but doubling gives only 0.80 / 1.40 = 0.57; tripling gives 0.67 and quadrupling 0.73.", "traps": {"a": "Lengthening the test 4 times gives 1.60 / 2.20 = 0.73, short of 0.80. Because of diminishing returns, more lengthening than this is needed.", "b": "Tripling the test gives 1.20 / 1.80 = 0.67, still well short of 0.80.", "c": "Doubling the test gives 0.80 / 1.40 = 0.57, not 0.80, because doubling the length does not double the reliability. Doubling is the case used in the split-half correction, which is why 2 tempts."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "concept": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention; it serves people who already have an addiction, so it is tertiary prevention. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, adapted the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention aims to stop a disorder from arising at all, lowering its incidence (new cases) through education, stress reduction, prenatal care, nutrition and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention aims at early detection and prompt treatment of cases that have already begun, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention aims to reduce the disability and relapse of people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention and rehabilitation. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Genetic screening and counselling identify carriers or risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Prenatal care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Arjun, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it largely ineffective, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "traps": {"a": "DARE (1983) is a school programme that teaches children to refuse drugs before they begin using them. That is primary prevention by design, whatever its later evaluations showed.", "b": "Genetic screening, with counselling, identifies carriers and at-risk pregnancies so that a disorder can be avoided before it begins, which is primary prevention in Caplan's scheme.", "d": "Prenatal health care (nutrition, infection control, avoiding alcohol and other teratogens) stops conditions such as intellectual disability from arising, the classic example of primary prevention."}}, {"q": "All of the following are true for multiple correlation coefficient R1.23 in a trivariate distribution EXCEPT:", "concept": "The answer is c: the statement that R1.23 ranges from minus 1 to plus 1 is false, because a multiple correlation coefficient is never negative. In a trivariate distribution, R1.23 is the multiple correlation between one variable, X1, and the best linear combination of the other two, X2 and X3. Least-squares regression finds the weights that make the predicted X1 values as close as possible to the actual ones, and R1.23 is simply the ordinary correlation between actual and predicted X1. Because the weights are chosen to maximise this correlation, any negative relation is absorbed into a negative weight, so R1.23 runs from 0 to 1. Its square is the proportion of variance in X1 explained by X2 and X3 together, and R1.23 is never smaller than r12 or r13 taken alone (ignoring sign). Suppose Meera predicts students' exam marks (X1) from study hours (X2) and sleep (X3) and gets R1.23 = 0.60: the two together explain 36% of the variance in marks. The boundary cases are statements b and d. If R1.23 = 0, X1 is uncorrelated with both X2 and X3, so r12, r13 and the partial correlations r12.3 and r13.2 are all zero. If R1.23 = 1, every point lies exactly on the regression plane and all residuals are zero. Option c is right because it gives R1.23 a negative range it cannot take. Exam trap: the range of Pearson's r (minus 1 to 1) is carried over by habit, but R measures only the size of an association, not its direction. Key note: option a is printed with strict signs (0 < R1.23 < 1), and strictly R can equal 0 or 1, as options b and d themselves discuss; a is the textbook '0 to 1' range with the equals signs lost in print, and the false statement the setter wants is c, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is the textbook range of R1.23, 0 to 1; the strict signs are a printing slip, since R can reach 0 or 1. It is a true property, so it is not the exception.", "b": "True: R1.23 = 0 means X1 has no linear relation with X2 or X3, so r12, r13 and the partials r12.3 and r13.2 are all zero. It is not the exception.", "d": "True: R1.23 = 1 means X1 is perfectly predicted from X2 and X3, every point lies on the regression plane, and all residuals are zero. It is not the exception."}}, {"q": "The only memory scale that can be used with both children and adults:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Wide Range Assessment of Memory and Learning, Second Edition (WRAML-2) is the memory battery normed for both children and adults. David Sheslow and Wayne Adams first published the WRAML in 1990 for ages 5 to 17. The second edition (2003) extended the norms to ages 5 to 90, so a single battery covers childhood through old age. Its core subtests pair into indices: Story Memory and Verbal Learning give a Verbal Memory Index, Design Memory and Picture Memory a Visual Memory Index, and Finger Windows and Number Letter an Attention/Concentration Index, which combine into a General Memory Index. Because the same tasks and score metric run across the whole age range, a clinician can compare memory profiles at any age on one scale. Kabir, aged 10, referred for a learning difficulty, and his grandmother, aged 70, referred after a mild stroke, could both be assessed with the WRAML-2. The other options were built for narrower groups. The Fuld Object Memory Evaluation (Paula Fuld, 1977) has older adults identify ten common objects by touch and recall them, to screen for dementia. The Rivermead Behavioural Memory Test (Barbara Wilson, Janet Cockburn and Alan Baddeley, 1985) assesses everyday memory, such as remembering a name, an appointment or a route, in adults with brain injury. The Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test (Andre Rey, 1941) is a 15-word list-learning test used mainly with adults. Option b is right because WRAML-2 was designed and normed to span children and adults in one instrument. Exam trap: the RAVLT is so widely used that it tempts, but it is a single list-learning test, not a child-and-adult battery. Key note: RAVLT and Rivermead have child adaptations or norms, so 'the only' is loose; WRAML-2 is the one battery built and normed for both in one test, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The Fuld Object Memory Evaluation (Paula Fuld, 1977) is a short object-recall test for older adults, used to screen for dementia. It is not normed for children.", "c": "The Rivermead Behavioural Memory Test (Barbara Wilson, Janet Cockburn and Alan Baddeley, 1985) measures everyday memory in adults with brain injury; children need a separate version, so it is not one scale for both.", "d": "The Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test (Andre Rey, 1941) is a 15-word list-learning test used mainly with adults; child norms exist, but it is a single test rather than a battery built for both age groups."}}, {"q": "The 'threshold method' of breaking a habit proposed by Guthrie is very much like:", "concept": "The answer is c: Edwin Guthrie's (1935) threshold method of breaking a habit is very much like systematic desensitisation. In The Psychology of Learning, Guthrie held a pure contiguity theory: a combination of stimuli that accompanied a movement will, when it recurs, tend to be followed by that movement, and the bond forms at full strength in a single trial. If a habit is just a cue tied to a response, it is broken by getting the cue to occur while some other response happens, so the new response becomes attached to it. Guthrie described three ways. In the threshold method the cue is introduced so faintly that it does not set off the unwanted response, then raised in small steps, always kept below the level that triggers it; a horse learns to accept a saddle when a light blanket is laid on its back first, then heavier blankets, then the saddle. In the fatigue method the response is provoked again and again until the animal is exhausted and stops. In the incompatible response method the cue is presented while a response that cannot occur together with the habit is going on. Option c is right because Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation works the same way: the client climbs a graded hierarchy of feared situations, from the least to the most frightening, each step kept weak enough that relaxation, not anxiety, wins. Meera, afraid of dogs, first looks at a photo of a puppy while relaxed, then watches a dog across the street, and only later pats one. Exam trap: desensitisation also pairs the cue with relaxation, an incompatible response (Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition), so students match it only to the incompatible response method; its graded, step-by-step hierarchy is what makes it the direct descendant of the threshold method.", "traps": {"a": "Assertiveness training (Andrew Salter, Joseph Wolpe) builds assertive responses that cannot coexist with anxiety, so it belongs with Guthrie's incompatible response method, not the graded threshold method.", "b": "Extinction removes the reinforcer (or the US) so a response fades; Guthrie denied that reinforcement mattered, and his threshold method replaces the old response with a new one rather than letting it die out.", "d": "Chaining (B. F. Skinner) links simple responses into a sequence, each step's stimulus setting the occasion for the next; it builds new behaviour rather than breaking a habit."}}, {"q": "The reinforcement centre in the brain:", "concept": "The answer is d: the nucleus accumbens is the brain's best-known reinforcement (reward) centre. It lies in the ventral striatum at the base of the forebrain, where the caudate nucleus and putamen join, and it receives the mesolimbic dopamine pathway from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain. The story began with James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), who found that rats with electrodes in the septal area would press a lever again and again, for hours, just to stimulate their own brains. Later mapping showed that the most rewarding sites lie along the medial forebrain bundle, which carries VTA dopamine fibres to the accumbens. Natural rewards such as food and sex, and almost every addictive drug, raise dopamine in the accumbens: cocaine and amphetamine act directly on dopamine terminals there, while opioids, nicotine and alcohol work more indirectly through the VTA. Dopamine here signals wanting and the prediction of reward more than pleasure itself. When Kabir hears his phone ping and feels the pull to check it at once, accumbens dopamine is part of that pull. Option d is right because the nucleus accumbens is the core of the brain's reinforcement circuit, the site where brain self-stimulation, natural rewards and drug reward meet. Exam trap: the options look like a matched set because each is a 'nucleus', but the basolateral and central nuclei belong to the amygdala (fear learning and fear output), and the basal nucleus of Meynert is the cholinergic cell group lost in Alzheimer's disease; none of them is the reward centre.", "traps": {"a": "The basal nucleus (nucleus basalis of Meynert) in the basal forebrain is the main source of acetylcholine to the cortex and degenerates in Alzheimer's disease; it serves attention and memory, not reward. Read as the amygdala's basal nucleus, it is part of the basolateral fear-learning complex, still not the reward centre.", "b": "The basolateral nucleus is the amygdala's input zone, where a tone and a shock become linked in fear conditioning; it gives cues emotional value but is not the reinforcement centre.", "c": "The central nucleus is the amygdala's output, sending signals to the hypothalamus and brainstem for the freezing, heart-rate and hormone changes of fear; it is a fear-expression centre."}}, {"q": "The word 'currency' is appropriate in the context of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the word 'currency' belongs to the anal personality. In Sigmund Freud's (1905) psychosexual theory, the anal stage runs from about 18 months to 3 years, when pleasure centres on holding back and expelling faeces and toilet training is the first big clash between an instinct and social demands. In 'Character and Anal Erotism' (1908) Freud described the adult anal character by a triad of traits: orderly, parsimonious (careful or stingy with money) and obstinate. He argued that the child's interest in faeces is later displaced on to money, so money, gold and currency become symbols of the stool the child once held back or handed over as a 'gift'. He pointed to old myths and folk sayings that link gold with dirt and excrement as support. A child who fights toilet training by holding back may become anal-retentive: neat, punctual, miserly, a hoarder of things and of money. A child who fights by soiling may become anal-expulsive: messy, wasteful and careless with money. Arjun, who counts every rupee, files every receipt in labelled folders and will not lend money to friends, fits the anal-retentive picture. Option b is right because Freud tied money (currency) directly to the anal stage through the symbolic link between faeces and money. Exam trap: 'currency' sounds like greed or getting things, which students link to the oral stage's 'taking in'; but oral traits concern dependence, eating, smoking and talking, while saving, hoarding and stinginess with money are the anal mark.", "traps": {"a": "Oral personalities (oral-incorporative and oral-aggressive) are marked by dependence, gullibility, eating, smoking and biting sarcasm; their symbols are food and taking in, not money.", "c": "Phallic personalities, from the Oedipal stage (about 3 to 6 years), show vanity, pride, recklessness and sexual display; money has no special symbolic place in this stage.", "d": "The genital character is Freud's mature ideal, able to love and to work; it is not tied to any special symbol such as money."}}, {"q": "If the mean IQ of Indians is 100 with a standard deviation of 16, what percentage of Indians would you expect to score at or below 132 on an IQ test?", "concept": "The answer is c: about 98% of people would score at or below 132. To read a score on a normal curve, first turn it into a z score, the number of standard deviations it lies from the mean: z = (X minus mean) / SD = (132 minus 100) / 16 = 32 / 16 = +2.0. Then use the known areas of the normal curve. About 34.1% of cases lie between the mean and 1 SD on each side, about 13.6% between 1 and 2 SD, and about 2.1% between 2 and 3 SD. Half the curve, 50%, lies below the mean. So the area at or below z = +2 is 50 + 34.1 + 13.6 = 97.7%, which rounds to 98%. Put another way, an IQ of 132 on this scale sits at about the 98th percentile, and only about 2% of people score higher. Option c is right because 132 lies exactly two standard deviations above the mean, and about 98% of a normal distribution falls at or below +2 SD. The SD of 16 is not a misprint: it was the convention of the Stanford-Binet deviation IQ from 1960 until the fifth edition (2003), while the Wechsler scales use 15; with an SD of 15 the same score of 132 would give z of about 2.13, a little higher. Exam trap: each wrong option is a real normal-curve figure attached to a different question. 84% is the area at or below +1 SD (an IQ of 116 here); 68% is the area within 1 SD on both sides (84 to 116); 34% is the area from the mean to +1 SD. Always check whether a question asks for the area below a score, between two scores or within a band around the mean.", "traps": {"a": "34% is the area between the mean and 1 SD above it (100 to 116 here); it leaves out the 50% below the mean and the band from 1 to 2 SD.", "b": "68% is the area within 1 SD either side of the mean (84 to 116); it describes the middle of the distribution, not the share at or below 132.", "d": "84% is the share at or below +1 SD, an IQ of 116 with this SD; 132 is two SDs above the mean, so the share is larger."}}, {"q": "A newly constructed test of creativity has a +0.15 correlation with four other standardised creativity scales and a correlation of +1.00 between the scores of any student who takes the test twice. This indicates that the test has:", "concept": "The answer is d: the test has low criterion validity but high reliability. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same result each time? A test-retest correlation of +1.00 means every student got the same score both times, which is perfect reliability (stability). Validity is accuracy: does the test measure what it claims to measure? One way to check is criterion-related validity, correlating the new test with an outside standard. When the standard is a set of established tests of the same trait given at about the same time, the result is concurrent validity, and in the multitrait-multimethod terms of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) it is also evidence of convergent validity. Here the new test correlates only +0.15 with four standard creativity scales, so it shares very little with them: whatever it measures so consistently, it is probably not creativity as those scales define it. Option d is right because a near-zero correlation with accepted criterion measures means low criterion validity, while the perfect retest correlation means high reliability. The case shows the classic rule that reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. A bathroom scale that always reads 5 kg too light is perfectly reliable but not valid. Exam trap: students see 'high reliability' and jump to option c, but face validity is judged simply by looking at the items (does it look like a creativity test to those who take it?), so no correlation can show it is low. The same holds for content validity in option a, which experts judge by inspecting how well the items cover the domain; and a's 'low reliability' is wrong in any case.", "traps": {"a": "Content validity is judged by experts checking whether the items sample the whole domain, not by correlations; and a retest correlation of +1.00 is the opposite of low reliability.", "b": "A correlation of +0.15 is weak, not moderate, evidence of validity against outside measures, and +1.00 is perfect, not moderate, reliability; 'external validity' is in any case a research design term for how far results generalise.", "c": "The reliability half is right, but face validity is whether the test looks suitable to the people taking it; it is judged by inspection, so these correlations say nothing about it."}}, {"q": "All of the following are characteristic features of conversion disorder, EXCEPT:", "concept": "The answer is d: a gradual, planned response to conflict is not a feature of conversion disorder. Conversion disorder, called functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5, is the old 'hysteria': a person loses or changes a voluntary motor or sensory function (weakness or paralysis of a limb, tremor, non-epileptic seizures, blindness, numbness, loss of voice) although no neurological disease explains it. Examination finds signs that do not fit anatomy, such as glove anaesthesia that stops sharply at the wrist or Hoover's sign in a 'paralysed' leg. The classic textbook features are these: the symptoms affect voluntary motor or sensory functions; they are not intentionally produced (non-volitional), so the person is not faking; onset is often sudden, soon after a stressor or conflict; and some patients show la belle indifference, a calm unconcern about a dramatic disability, a phrase usually credited to Pierre Janet (1859 to 1947). Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) saw the symptom as an unconscious conflict 'converted' into a bodily one, bringing primary gain (the conflict stays out of awareness) and secondary gain (sympathy, escape from duties). When Ananya suddenly cannot move her right arm on the morning of a board exam she dreads, and no nerve or brain damage is found, that is the picture. Option d is right because conversion is an unconscious, usually abrupt reaction, while a gradual, planned, deliberate response points to factitious disorder (to take on the sick role) or malingering (for external gain). Exam trap: DSM-5 warns that la belle indifference should not be used to make the diagnosis, because it is not specific and also occurs in real neurological illness, yet textbooks still list it as a characteristic feature, so option b is a feature and not the exception.", "traps": {"a": "This is the defining feature: conversion symptoms involve voluntary motor or sensory functions (paralysis, blindness, seizures, anaesthesia) that neurological disease cannot explain.", "b": "La belle indifference, the calm unconcern about a serious symptom (a phrase usually credited to Pierre Janet), is a classic (though neither specific nor required) feature, so it is not the exception.", "c": "Non-volitional aetiology is central: the symptom is not consciously produced, which is what separates conversion from factitious disorder and malingering."}}, {"q": "In Jellinek's typology of alcoholism, deliberate, undisciplined drinking is a characteristic of:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Elvin Morton Jellinek's (1960) typology, deliberate, undisciplined drinking marks alpha alcoholics. In The Disease Concept of Alcoholism, Jellinek sorted problem drinkers into five 'species' named with Greek letters. Alpha alcoholism is purely psychological dependence: the person drinks deliberately to relieve bodily or emotional pain, worry or tension. The drinking is 'undisciplined' in that it breaks the social rules about when, where and how much people drink, and it can strain family and work, but there is no loss of control, no inability to abstain and no withdrawal, and it need not get worse. Beta alcoholism brings physical complications such as cirrhosis, gastritis or nerve damage from heavy drinking, without physical or psychological dependence. Gamma alcoholism, the type most common in Anglo-American countries and in Alcoholics Anonymous, shows tolerance, withdrawal, craving and loss of control: once started, the person cannot stop. Delta alcoholism, common in wine cultures such as France, shows inability to abstain even for a day, though the amount at each sitting stays controlled. Epsilon alcoholism is periodic or binge drinking. Jellinek counted only gamma and delta as true diseases. Ravi, who reaches for whisky every evening to calm his work anxiety and drinks more than his circle thinks proper, yet can stop when he chooses and has no withdrawal, fits alpha. Option a is right because alpha alcoholism is defined by deliberate, relief-seeking drinking that breaks social discipline without loss of control. Exam trap: 'loss of control' (gamma) and 'inability to abstain' (delta) are the two most often swapped; the word 'deliberate' signals that control is intact, which rules out both.", "traps": {"b": "Beta alcoholism is heavy drinking that causes physical complications (cirrhosis, gastritis, polyneuropathy) without physical or psychological dependence; it is defined by bodily damage, not by deliberate relief drinking.", "c": "Gamma alcoholism shows tolerance, withdrawal and loss of control (once started, the drinker cannot stop); Jellinek saw it as the classic disease form, the opposite of deliberate drinking.", "d": "Delta alcoholism is the inability to abstain even for a day while the amount each time stays controlled, typical of wine-drinking cultures; it is a form of physical dependence."}}, {"q": "A post-reinforcement pause is a salient feature of which of the following schedule of reinforcement?", "concept": "The answer is c: the post-reinforcement pause is a salient feature of fixed interval and fixed ratio schedules. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses earn a reward. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, mapped four basic partial schedules by crossing ratio (reward after a number of responses) with interval (reward for the first response after a period of time), each either fixed or variable. A post-reinforcement pause is a break in responding just after each reinforcer. It appears when the schedule is fixed, because the animal can in effect tell that the next reward is not available yet. On a fixed interval schedule the pause is followed by a gradual speeding up as the time for the next reward nears, giving the scalloped curve on a cumulative record. On a fixed ratio schedule the animal pauses, then runs off the whole ratio at a high, steady rate, the break-and-run pattern; the pause grows longer as the ratio gets larger. On variable schedules the next reward could follow the very next response or come at the next moment, so there is no safe time to rest, and responding is steady with little or no pausing. Priya, paid a fixed amount for every 50 envelopes she packs, takes a short break after each batch before starting the next: a fixed ratio pause. Option c is right because both fixed schedules, and only they, produce a reliable pause after reinforcement. Exam trap: every wrong option contains a variable schedule, and variable ratio, which appears in two of them (twice in b), is the schedule famous for the highest, steadiest rate with almost no pausing.", "traps": {"a": "Fixed interval does give a pause (the scallop), but variable ratio gives the highest, steadiest rate with almost no pause, as on a slot machine.", "b": "Variable ratio, named twice, produces steady, rapid responding with little pausing because any response might pay; it is the schedule least known for a pause.", "d": "Fixed ratio does give the break-and-run pause, but variable interval gives a moderate, very steady rate without pauses, since a reward may become available at any moment."}}, {"q": "The neural basis for non-associative learning (viz., habituation and sensitisation) can be explained by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the neural basis of habituation and sensitisation is explained by changes in synaptic facilitation. Non-associative learning is a change in the response to a single kind of stimulus, without pairing it with anything else. In habituation the response to a repeated harmless stimulus weakens; in sensitisation a strong or noxious stimulus makes responses to many other stimuli stronger. Eric Kandel (from the 1960s; Nobel Prize 2000) traced both to identified synapses in the sea slug Aplysia. A light touch to its siphon makes it withdraw its gill. With repeated touches the sensory neurons release less glutamate onto the motor neurons (homosynaptic depression, the reverse of facilitation), so the reflex habituates. A shock to the tail activates facilitating interneurons that release serotonin onto the sensory neuron terminals; serotonin raises cyclic AMP and protein kinase A, prolongs the action potential and increases transmitter release. This presynaptic facilitation makes the gill withdrawal stronger: sensitisation. The strength of one synapse is thus turned down or up, and facilitation is the textbook name for the mechanism. Sneha stops noticing the ceiling fan after a few minutes (habituation) but jumps at every small sound after a near-accident on the road (sensitisation). Option b is right because non-associative learning in Aplysia rests on presynaptic facilitation (and its mirror image, synaptic depression) at the sensory-to-motor synapse, not on new links between stimuli. Exam trap: long-term potentiation is also a change in synaptic strength, so it tempts students, but it was found in the hippocampus by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) and is the model for associative learning, because it depends on the pre- and postsynaptic cells being active together.", "traps": {"a": "Long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo, 1973) is a lasting strengthening of hippocampal synapses after high-frequency stimulation; because the NMDA receptor needs paired activity, it models associative learning and memory, not habituation.", "c": "Generalised imitation is an operant idea from Donald Baer and colleagues (1967): once imitating is itself reinforced, a child copies new models without direct reward; it is a behavioural concept, not a neural mechanism.", "d": "Equipotentiality is Karl Lashley's (1929) claim, with mass action, that any intact part of a functional cortical area can carry out a learned function after another part is damaged; it concerns where memories are stored, not how simple reflexes change."}}, {"q": "Allport's concept of functional autonomy resembles the Freudian notion of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Gordon Allport's (1937) functional autonomy resembles the Freudian notion of sublimation. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, Allport argued that adult motives can become independent of the needs that first started them. A sailor first goes to sea to earn a living; years later, rich and retired, he still longs for the sea for its own sake. Allport later (1961) split this into perseverative functional autonomy (low-level habits and routines that persist) and propriate functional autonomy (motives bound up with the self and its values). Sublimation, for Sigmund Freud (1905), is the healthiest way of handling an impulse, often called the most mature defence: an unacceptable sexual or aggressive impulse is redirected into a socially valued activity such as art, science, sport or surgery. Option c is right because in both ideas a mature, valued adult activity looks quite unlike the need it grew out of, and in both the present motive is a transformed descendant of an older one. The difference is the fuel. Freud held that the sublimated activity is still driven by the original libidinal or aggressive energy, now disguised; Allport held that the new motive runs on its own and no longer needs its root. Rohan, who began learning the tabla to please his father and now practises at midnight because he loves it, illustrates Allport. Exam trap: knowing that Allport rejected Freud's account of adult motives, students think no Freudian idea can resemble functional autonomy, but the question asks only which one looks most alike. Key note: under the options the paper prints a stray line, 'motives or desires and the current expressed behaviors or attitudes', the tail of a stem about a break between original motives and current behaviour; reaction formation also shows such a break, but it turns a desire into its opposite and keeps the original desire active and hidden, which is far from functional autonomy; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Intellectualisation, listed by Anna Freud (1936), handles a painful feeling by flight into abstract, detached thinking about it; it is a defence against emotion, not a motive that becomes independent.", "b": "Introjection is taking in the values or traits of others, especially parents, as one's own, which builds the superego; it concerns how standards are acquired, not how motives change over time.", "d": "Reaction formation turns an unacceptable desire into its exaggerated opposite (over-sweetness hiding hostility); the original desire stays active and hidden, the reverse of a motive that has become free of its origin."}}, {"q": "Kandel's studies of the sea slug aplysia were used to support the hypothesis that:", "concept": "The answer is c: Eric Kandel's studies of Aplysia (from the 1960s onwards; Nobel Prize 2000, shared with Arvid Carlsson and Paul Greengard) supported the hypothesis that basic changes in neural pathways occur with learning and memory. Kandel chose the sea slug Aplysia because its nervous system has only about 20,000 neurons, many large enough to be identified and recorded one by one in every animal. He studied the gill-withdrawal reflex: touch the siphon and the gill pulls in. He showed that habituation, sensitisation and even classical conditioning of this reflex are stored as changes in the strength of specific synapses between sensory neurons, interneurons and motor neurons. Short-term memory, lasting minutes, uses changes in existing proteins: serotonin raises cyclic AMP and protein kinase A, which increases transmitter release. Long-term memory, lasting days, needs new gene expression: repeated training sends protein kinase A into the nucleus, where it switches on CREB, new proteins are made, and the sensory neuron grows new synaptic connections (or loses some, in long-term habituation). This was some of the first direct cellular evidence that learning physically changes the wiring of a nervous system, an idea earlier proposed by Santiago Ramon y Cajal (1894) and Donald Hebb (1949). Option c is right because Kandel's whole programme showed that learning and memory are carried by changes in specific neural pathways. When Anjali practises a raga daily for months, the same principle applies: the synapses she uses grow stronger and more numerous. Exam trap: 'heritable' (option b) tempts students because Kandel showed that gene expression is involved, but switching genes on inside a neuron during learning does not pass the change to offspring.", "traps": {"a": "Insight learning is Wolfgang Kohler's (1925) sudden grasp of a problem's structure by chimpanzees; Aplysia shows only simple reflex learning (habituation, sensitisation, classical conditioning), nothing like insight.", "b": "Kandel showed that long-term memory needs gene expression in the neuron (CREB), but these changes happen within one animal's synapses and are not passed to its offspring, so they are not heritable.", "d": "Proprioception, the sense of body position, relies on muscle spindles and joint receptors, not chemical cues; Kandel's work was about synaptic change in learning, not about proprioception."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is due to the regeneration of rhodopsin?", "concept": "The answer is d: dark adaptation is due to the regeneration of rhodopsin. Rhodopsin is the light-sensitive pigment of the rods, the receptors for dim-light (scotopic) vision. It is made of a protein, opsin, joined to retinal, a form of vitamin A. When a photon strikes it, retinal changes shape and the molecule breaks apart (bleaches), which starts the neural signal; a bleached molecule cannot respond again until it is rebuilt, and rebuilding is slow. In bright daylight most rhodopsin is bleached, so the rods contribute almost nothing. Walk into a dark cinema and at first you see nothing. Over the next minutes sensitivity rises in two stages: the cones adapt quickly and level off after about 5 to 10 minutes; then comes a kink in the curve, the rod-cone break, and the rods, as their rhodopsin regenerates, take over and keep improving until full sensitivity at about 20 to 30 minutes, when the eye can be many thousands of times more sensitive than at the start. Vitamin A deficiency slows rhodopsin regeneration and causes night blindness. When Tara enters a dark auditorium she stumbles at first and later reads the seat numbers: that is her rhodopsin coming back. Option d is right because the slow return of sensitivity in the dark is caused by the rebuilding of the rod pigment. Exam trap: light adaptation is the paired process, so students choose it; but it is the fast fall in sensitivity on entering bright light, caused by bleaching of pigment, the opposite of regeneration.", "traps": {"a": "Lateral inhibition is the way neighbouring retinal cells suppress one another, sharpening edges and producing Mach bands, as H. Keffer Hartline showed in the Limulus eye; it is a wiring effect, not pigment chemistry.", "b": "Light adaptation is the rapid drop in sensitivity on entering bright light, mainly from pigment bleaching and a shift to cone vision; it is the opposite of rhodopsin regeneration.", "c": "Stereopsis is depth perception from binocular disparity, the slightly different images in the two eyes; it depends on the visual cortex comparing the two eyes, not on photopigments."}}, {"q": "Regions and boundaries are central to personality theory of:", "concept": "The answer is c: regions and boundaries are central to the personality theory of Kurt Lewin (1936). In Principles of Topological Psychology, Lewin built a field theory of personality and behaviour out of Gestalt ideas. His formula B = f(P, E) says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment, which together make up the life space: all the facts that influence a person at one moment, as that person sees them. Lewin borrowed topology, a branch of geometry that deals with spatial relations, to draw the life space as a map of regions. Each region is an area of possible activity (studying, friends, family) or a part of the person, who is divided into inner-personal regions (needs) and a perceptual-motor region that deals with the world. Regions are separated by boundaries that can be firm or permeable: across a firm boundary, tension in one region does not spill into the next. Moving from one region to another is called locomotion; regions carry positive or negative valences, forces are drawn as vectors, and barriers block paths. As a child grows, the life space becomes more differentiated, with more regions. Divya, who wants to join a dance class (a positive valence) but must first get past her parents' refusal (a barrier), is a classic Lewinian picture. Option c is right because the language of regions, boundaries, locomotion, valences and vectors belongs to Lewin's topological psychology. Exam trap: George Kelly's (1955) personal construct theory also uses spatial-sounding terms such as 'range of convenience', but its units are bipolar constructs, not regions and boundaries.", "traps": {"a": "George Kelly's personal construct theory sees each person as a scientist who interprets events through bipolar constructs (kind versus cruel), measured with the Role Construct Repertory Test; it maps constructs, not regions.", "b": "Karen Horney built her theory on basic anxiety and three neurotic trends (moving toward, against and away from people); it is about interpersonal strategies, not topological regions.", "d": "David McClelland studied the needs for achievement, power and affiliation, measured with TAT stories; his is a motive theory, not a field theory of regions and boundaries."}}, {"q": "Autistic disorder is linked to a deficiency in:", "concept": "The answer is d: autistic disorder has been linked to a deficiency in the mirror neuron system. Mirror neurons were found by Giacomo Rizzolatti and colleagues in the 1990s in area F5 of the macaque's ventral premotor cortex: the same cells fired when the monkey grasped a piece of food and when it watched a person grasp one. In humans a mirror system is described in the inferior frontal gyrus (including Broca's area), premotor cortex and inferior parietal lobule. Because it maps another person's action on to one's own motor plan, it was proposed as a basis for imitation, understanding others' intentions and empathy. Autism involves difficulties in exactly these areas: poor imitation, weak joint attention and trouble reading other minds. This led to the 'broken mirror' hypothesis. Lindsay Oberman, Vilayanur Ramachandran and colleagues (2005) reported that the EEG mu rhythm, which is suppressed when typical people watch actions, was not suppressed in autistic participants, and Mirella Dapretto and colleagues (2006) found less inferior frontal mirror activity when autistic children imitated emotional faces, with the least activity in the most impaired children. Six-year-old Ishaan, who does not copy his teacher's clapping game or smile back at her, shows the kind of imitation gap the theory set out to explain. Option d is right because, of these options, the mirror neuron system is the one specifically proposed as deficient to explain autism's imitation and social difficulties. Exam trap: mirror neurons fire for both doing and watching an action, which is why a motor system came to be linked to a social disorder; the amygdala, a genuine rival account, is the most tempting wrong choice. Key note: Simon Baron-Cohen's amygdala theory (2000) also links autism to the amygdala, and the broken mirror account is now disputed, since many later studies find intact imitation and mirror responses in autism; the question uses the mirror neuron account; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala, which tags faces and events with emotional meaning, is the basis of a rival account (Simon Baron-Cohen's amygdala theory of autism, 2000), but the 'deficiency' link this question tests is the broken mirror hypothesis.", "b": "The cingulate gyrus, part of the limbic system, handles conflict monitoring, error detection and the emotional side of pain; it is not the structure singled out as deficient in autism.", "c": "The hippocampus serves the forming of new declarative memories and spatial maps (H.M., place cells); autism is not defined by a memory deficit, so it is not the textbook link."}}, {"q": "The onset of dreams is due to the increased activity of:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the classic account, the onset of dreaming (REM) sleep is due to increased activity of the locus coeruleus. Dreams occur mostly in REM sleep, discovered by Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953): rapid eye movements, a waking-like EEG and loss of muscle tone, with vivid, story-like dreams reported on waking. Michel Jouvet, working on cats in Lyon from the late 1950s, named it paradoxical sleep and showed (1962) that it is generated in the pons: cats with the forebrain removed still cycled into it. In his monoamine theory of sleep, serotonin from the raphe nuclei starts slow-wave sleep, and the locus coeruleus, a small blue-pigmented nucleus in the dorsal pons that is the brain's main source of noradrenaline, triggers paradoxical sleep, the phase of vivid dreams. Jouvet's lesions in the locus coeruleus region abolished the muscle atonia of REM, so the cats got up during that sleep and seemed to act out their dreams, stalking and attacking unseen prey. When Nikhil's eyes start darting under his lids about 90 minutes after he falls asleep, he has entered the first REM period of the night. Option a is right because, in Jouvet's textbook account, rising locus coeruleus activity switches the brain from slow-wave sleep into REM sleep, when dreams begin. Exam trap: the raphe nuclei are the other half of the same theory, but they start slow-wave sleep, not dreaming. Key note: modern work (J. Allan Hobson and Robert McCarley, 1975 reciprocal interaction model) shows locus coeruleus noradrenaline and raphe serotonin neurons fall silent in REM ('REM-off'), while cholinergic pontine neurons (PPT, LDT) and the sublaterodorsal (subcoeruleus) area beside the locus coeruleus are 'REM-on'; the question follows Jouvet's textbook account; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The raphe nuclei, the brain's serotonin source, were the other half of Michel Jouvet's monoamine theory: their activity was held to start slow-wave (non-REM) sleep, not the onset of dreaming.", "c": "The caudate nuclei are part of the basal ganglia (the striatum), serving movement selection, habits and learning; they play no part in switching on REM sleep.", "d": "The hippocampus shows theta rhythm during REM and helps consolidate memories in sleep, but it does not trigger dream onset; it is a target of REM activity, not its generator."}}, {"q": "A non-invasive procedure capable of revealing the neural activity of the regions of the brain:", "concept": "The answer is b: functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) is the non-invasive procedure that reveals the neural activity of brain regions. MRI places the head in a strong magnetic field and uses radio waves to read signals from hydrogen nuclei, so no X-rays or other ionising radiation are involved. fMRI adds the time dimension. When a brain region becomes active, local blood flow rises more than the neurons' extra oxygen use, so the region ends up with relatively more oxygenated haemoglobin. Oxygenated and deoxygenated haemoglobin differ in their magnetic properties, and this difference gives the blood oxygenation level dependent (BOLD) signal described by Seiji Ogawa and colleagues (1990). Scanning every second or two while a person does a task shows which regions become more active. Nothing is injected and nothing is radioactive, so the method is non-invasive and can be repeated many times. Its spatial resolution is good (a few millimetres) but its timing is slow (seconds), because blood flow lags behind neural firing. When Aisha looks at photographs of faces in the scanner, the BOLD signal rises in her fusiform face area. Option b is right because fMRI is the only option that both shows activity, not just structure, and needs no injection or radiation. Exam trap: PET also shows brain activity, so students pick option a; but PET needs a radioactive tracer such as fluorodeoxyglucose injected into the blood, so it is invasive. The two structural scans, CT and plain MRI, are the other trap: MRI is non-invasive but shows only anatomy.", "traps": {"a": "Positron emission tomography (Michael Phelps and colleagues, 1970s) does show regional activity, but it needs a radioactive tracer injected into the blood, so it is invasive.", "c": "Computerised tomography (Godfrey Hounsfield, 1972) builds cross-sectional X-ray images; it shows structure such as tumours or bleeding, uses ionising radiation and shows no neural activity.", "d": "Structural MRI is non-invasive and gives sharp anatomical pictures, but on its own it shows the brain's structure, not its moment-to-moment activity; that needs the functional (BOLD) version."}}, {"q": "An antagonist to AcetylCholine (Ach):", "concept": "The answer is b: curare is an antagonist to acetylcholine (ACh). An antagonist is a drug that blocks or reduces the action of a neurotransmitter, often by sitting on its receptor without activating it. ACh is the transmitter at the neuromuscular junction, where motor neurons make skeletal muscle contract, and there it acts on nicotinic receptors, which are ion channels. Curare, an arrow poison of South American hunters, is a plant extract whose main active part, d-tubocurarine, binds to these nicotinic receptors and keeps ACh off them, so the muscles cannot respond. The result is limp paralysis; death comes from paralysis of the breathing muscles while the mind stays clear. Hunters could still eat the prey safely because curare is poorly absorbed from the gut. Claude Bernard (1850s) showed with frog nerve-muscle preparations that curare acts at the junction between nerve and muscle, not on the nerve or the muscle itself. Curare-like drugs later became muscle relaxants in surgery and in modified electroconvulsive therapy. Other ACh antagonists act elsewhere: atropine and scopolamine block muscarinic receptors, and botulinum toxin works differently again, by stopping ACh release. Option b is right because curare is the classic textbook example of a drug that blocks acetylcholine at its nicotinic receptors. Exam trap: clonidine lowers activity in a transmitter system, so it sounds like an antagonist, but it is an agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors that reduces noradrenaline release; and Metrazol acts on the GABA system, not on ACh.", "traps": {"a": "Tropisetron is a serotonin 5-HT3 receptor antagonist used to prevent nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy; it blocks serotonin, not acetylcholine.", "c": "Metrazol (pentylenetetrazol) blocks the GABA-A receptor and causes seizures; Ladislas Meduna began chemical convulsive therapy in 1934 with camphor and soon switched to Metrazol, so it is a GABA antagonist and convulsant, not an ACh blocker.", "d": "Clonidine is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist that reduces noradrenaline release; it is used for high blood pressure, opioid withdrawal and ADHD, not as an acetylcholine blocker."}}, {"q": "Which statements are true about Reliability and Validity: 1. Reliability and validity are two dimensions of test efficiency 2. The requirements of high reliability and high validity are opposite to each other 3. The validity of the test is always higher than the index of reliability 4. Validity may be high even without high reliability. Choose the correct option:", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 2 are correct (option c is printed '1, 2 and 2', meaning 1 and 2 only). Statement 1 is true: reliability (consistency of scores) and validity (accuracy for the intended purpose) are the two basic dimensions of a test's efficiency, the twin standards every test manual must report. Statement 2 is true in a specific technical sense, the attenuation paradox discussed by Harold Gulliksen (1950) and Jane Loevinger (1954). To push internal consistency up, a test maker keeps items that correlate highly with one another, so the test becomes narrow and homogeneous. To push validity against a broad, complex criterion (job success, school achievement) up, the best items are those that each correlate with the criterion but only weakly with one another, so each adds something new. Beyond a point, then, the two demands pull in opposite directions. Statement 3 is false: validity can never exceed the index of reliability, the square root of the reliability coefficient, because a test cannot correlate with anything else more than it correlates with its own true score. If Kavya's new aptitude test has a reliability of 0.81, its validity cannot exceed 0.90. Statement 4 is false: a test full of random error cannot correlate well with anything, so it cannot be highly valid without being reliable; reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps 1 and 2 and drops both false statements, 3 and 4. Exam trap: statement 3 reverses the true rule; the index of reliability is the ceiling for validity, not its floor. Key note: option c is printed '1, 2 and 2 are correct', plainly a misprint for '1 and 2 are correct'; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Statements 1 and 2 are right, but statement 4 is false: a test that is mostly random error cannot correlate highly with any criterion, so validity cannot be high without reliability.", "b": "Statements 1 and 2 are right, but statement 3 reverses the rule: validity is capped by the index of reliability (the square root of the reliability coefficient) and can never exceed it.", "d": "This drops the true statement 2 and keeps both false ones: validity cannot exceed the index of reliability (statement 3) or be high without reliability (statement 4)."}}, {"q": "Match List I (Neurological condition) with List II (Brain site): A. Finger agnosia, B. Dressing apraxia, C. General amnesic syndrome, D. Intermanual conflict. 1. Bilateral medial temporal lesions, 2. Right posterior parieto-temporal area, 3. Left posterior brain, 4. Cerebral commissurotomy", "concept": "The answer is b: A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4. Finger agnosia (A) is the inability to name, point to or tell apart one's own or another person's fingers. It follows damage to the left posterior brain, at the angular gyrus of the parietal lobe, Josef Gerstmann (1924) first described it, and it became the core of his syndrome with agraphia, acalculia and left-right confusion: so A-3. Dressing apraxia (B) is difficulty putting on clothes, with sleeves, buttons and the left and right sides muddled, from a breakdown of spatial and body-schema processing. It goes with right posterior parieto-temporal damage, often alongside left-sided neglect and constructional apraxia: so B-2. The general amnesic syndrome (C), a dense anterograde amnesia with intact intelligence and short-term memory, follows bilateral medial temporal lesions of the hippocampus and nearby cortex, as in the patient H.M. after William Scoville's (1953) operation, studied by Brenda Milner (1957): so C-1. Intermanual conflict (D), in which one hand undoes what the other does (one buttons a shirt while the other unbuttons it), is a callosal sign after cerebral commissurotomy, the split-brain operation studied by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s), and is a form of alien hand behaviour: so D-4. When Ramesh, after a right parietal stroke, puts his shirt on inside out and cannot find the left sleeve, that is dressing apraxia. Option b is right because it gives exactly these four pairs. Exam trap: the two parietal syndromes are easily swapped, so remember that finger agnosia is left (with the language-linked Gerstmann signs) and dressing apraxia is right (with spatial neglect); option a makes exactly that swap.", "traps": {"a": "This swaps A and B: finger agnosia is the left posterior (Gerstmann) sign and dressing apraxia the right parieto-temporal one, not the reverse.", "c": "A-3 is right, but dressing apraxia is not a split-brain sign, amnesia does not come from the right parieto-temporal area, and intermanual conflict is not a medial temporal lesion effect.", "d": "A-3 and C-1 are right, but it gives dressing apraxia to commissurotomy and intermanual conflict to the right parieto-temporal area, reversing the true B and D sites."}}]});
